== 1 The problem and the result <sec:introduction>

The exact molecular electronic Hamiltonian has unusually rigid input data: its external potential is generated by a list of positive nuclei. A hardness proof for a matrix obtained by projecting onto supplied orbitals does not by itself establish hardness for this operator. Projection provides an upper bound on the ground energy, while a reduction also needs a lower bound that excludes all omitted continuum states. We give such a reduction for the integer-charge, rational-position encoding below.

=== 1.1 The precise promise problem <the-precise-promise-problem>

Fix a positive integer $c$. An input consists of distinct positions $R_1 \, dots.h \, R_M in bb(Q)^3$, positive integers $Z_1 \, dots.h \, Z_M$, an electron number $N gt.eq 1$, and rational thresholds $a < b$. Here $M gt.eq 1$, $N$ is encoded in unary, and the charges are encoded in binary. Every coordinate and threshold is encoded as a signed binary numerator and a positive binary denominator in lowest terms, using a fixed standard self-delimiting encoding. Write $L$ for the total bit length and require $b - a gt.eq L^(- c)$.

On $and.big^N L^2 \( bb(R)^3 \; bb(C)^2 \)$ consider, in atomic units,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.1)")
 $ H \( R \, Z \, N \) = - 1 / 2 sum_(ell = 1)^N Delta_(x_ell) - sum_(ell = 1)^N sum_(alpha = 1)^M frac(Z_alpha, \| x_ell - R_alpha \|) + sum_(1 lt.eq ell < k lt.eq N) frac(1, \| x_ell - x_k \|) . $ <eq:physical-hamiltonian>]

The form domain is

$ cal(Q)_N = H^1 \( \( bb(R)^3 \)^N \; \( bb(C)^2 \)^(⊗ N) \) ∩ and.big^N L^2 \( bb(R)^3 \; bb(C)^2 \) . $

Antisymmetry exchanges spatial and spin coordinates together. We use the lower-bounded self-adjoint Coulomb operator associated with the closed form on $cal(Q)_N$. The three-dimensional Hardy inequality, applied with other coordinates fixed, gives infinitesimal form bounds for the finitely many Coulomb terms and justifies this convention. Set

$ E_0 \( R \, Z \, N \) = inf "Spec" H \( R \, Z \, N \) . $

The YES promise is $E_0 lt.eq a$, and the NO promise is $E_0 gt.eq b$. No requirement is made outside the promise.

