#thm([Lemma 2.4.])[
  (Contact classification).  #emph[All the sites $p_i$ are distinct. Every pair is either at distance one or at distance at least $sqrt(2)$. The complete set $cal(C)$ of unit-distance pairs consists of:]
  + #emph[$L A_e$, $A_e R$, and $A_e B_e$ for a negative source edge;]
  + #emph[$L A_e$, $A_e B_e$, and $B_e R$ for a positive source edge;]
  + #emph[$B_e A_f$ for two consecutive positive source edges $e \, f$ on the same original lattice line.]
  #emph[Thus every interaction of $H_(+)$ is a contact. Its weight on a contact will be denoted $K_(i j) gt.eq 0$, using $K_(i j) = 0$ for contacts in the third class.]
] <lem:contact-layout>

#proof[
  Within one gadget the assertion follows directly from (2.8). For a negative gadget the two distances from $B_e$ to original endpoints are $sqrt(2)$; for a positive gadget the two nonadjacent endpoint–ancilla distances are $sqrt(3)$. Original vertices are mutually separated by at least $2$. An original vertex other than the endpoints of an ancilla’s gadget, on that same axis line, has along-line distance at least $3 \/ 2$ to a positive ancilla and at least $3$ to a negative midpoint. An original vertex off that line differs in a transverse coordinate by at least $2$.
  For ancillas belonging to distinct gadgets on the same axis line, the only along-line separation below $3 \/ 2$ is the separation $1$ between $B_e$ and $A_f$ for consecutive positive gadgets. They have the same height and hence are a contact. All remaining such pairs are at least $3 \/ 2 > sqrt(2)$ apart. Distinct parallel axis lines have transverse separation at least $2$.
  It remains to compare horizontal and vertical ancillas. Call an ancilla #emph[midpoint type] if it belongs to a negative gadget, and #emph[positive type] otherwise. Two midpoint-type projections differ by at least $1$ in each planar coordinate, so their distance is at least $sqrt(2)$. A midpoint-type and a positive-type projection differ by at least $1$ and $1 \/ 2$ in the two planar coordinates. Their heights differ by at least $sqrt(3) \/ 2$, since horizontal and vertical heights have opposite signs. Their squared distance is therefore at least $1 + 1 \/ 4 + 3 \/ 4 = 2$. Finally two positive-type ancillas from perpendicular directions have height difference $sqrt(3)$, already larger than $sqrt(2)$. These cases exhaust all pairs and also establish distinctness. ◻
]

Orient each contact arbitrarily and write $u_(i j) = p_j - p_i$, so $\| u_(i j) \| = 1$. Its first-order length change under site displacements $s_i$ is $u_(i j) dot.op \( s_j - s_i \)$. Reversing the orientation reverses both factors and leaves this scalar unchanged. The following stronger-than-counting fact is what permits independent hopping calibration.

#thm([Lemma 2.5.])[
  (A bounded right inverse for contact lengths).  #emph[For every real assignment $\( d_(i j) \)_({ i \, j } in cal(C))$ there are displacements $s_i in bb(R)^3$ such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.9)")
 $ u_(i j) dot.op \( s_j - s_i \) = d_(i j) \, #h(2em) max_i \| s_i \| lt.eq 6 m max_(cal(C)) \| d_(i j) \| . $ <eq:contact-displacement>]

 For an empty contact set take all displacements zero. The construction is linear in $d$, uses polynomially many arithmetic operations with coefficients in $bb(Q) \( sqrt(3) \)$, and has no consistency condition around lattice cycles.]
] <lem:displacement>

#proof[
  Set the height displacement of every original vertex to zero. The horizontal coordinate of each original vertex will be determined using only its original row, and its vertical planar coordinate using only its original column. These are independent variables even when both directions meet at the same vertex.
  Fix one such axis line. Write $a$ for displacement along it, $z$ for physical height displacement, and put $sigma = 1$ for a horizontal line and $sigma = - 1$ for a vertical line. On every connected run of negative bonds choose the first original $a$ coordinate to be zero, and accumulate
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.10)")
   $ a_R & = a_L + d_(L A_e) + d_(A_e R) \, #h(2em) a_(A_e) = a_L + d_(L A_e) \,\
a_(B_e) & = a_(A_e) \, #h(2em) z_(A_e) = 0 \, #h(2em) z_(B_e) = sigma d_(A_e B_e) . $ <eq:negative-displacement>]
  Unconstrained original coordinates may be set to zero. A positive or missing bond places no requirement on the difference of its original endpoints’ $a$ coordinates at this step, so it separates runs. Each run is a path on a line, and (2.10) satisfies all three constraints in its negative gadgets.
  Now consider the elevated vertices of positive gadgets on this line. Their same-height contacts, including the extra contacts of Lemma 5, form disjoint paths. Set the first $a$ coordinate on each path to zero and successively set $a_(upright("next")) = a_(upright("current")) + d_(upright("edge"))$. Every such contact vector is along the axis, so its constraint involves only these $a$ coordinates. Each elevated vertex has exactly one additional, sloped contact to an original vertex. Writing $h_0 = sqrt(3) \/ 2$, set
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.11)")
   $ z_(A_e) & = frac(d_(L A_e) - \( a_(A_e) - a_L \) \/ 2, sigma h_0) \,\
z_(B_e) & = frac(d_(B_e R) - \( a_R - a_(B_e) \) \/ 2, sigma h_0) . $ <eq:positive-displacement>]
  Indeed the two unit spoke vectors are $1 / 2 e_(upright("axis")) + sigma h_0 e_z$ and $1 / 2 e_(upright("axis")) - sigma h_0 e_z$, respectively. Because the original height displacements vanish, these formulas satisfy the remaining equations without imposing any new condition on original coordinates. Set each ancilla’s unused transverse coordinate to zero.
  Repeat on all rows and columns. The row procedure changes only original $x$ coordinates, and the column procedure only original $y$ coordinates. No ancilla belongs to both procedures. Thus the constructions do not overwrite each other. In particular a square cycle is not a cycle in either scalar accumulation graph; its vector closure is automatic because actual vertex displacements have been assigned.
  For the norm bound put $d_max = max_(cal(C)) \| d_(i j) \|$. Every original along-line coordinate has magnitude at most $2 m d_max$, and every accumulated elevated coordinate has magnitude at most $m d_max$. Negative ancilla coordinates have magnitude at most $\( 2 m + 1 \) d_max$ along the line and at most $d_max$ in height. From (2.11), positive ancilla heights are at most $\( 1 + 3 m \/ 2 \) d_max \/ h_0$. Combining at most two nonzero coordinates at any site gives the stated bound $6 m d_max$. All assignments are additions and multiplication by fixed rational numbers or by $1 \/ h_0$, proving the algorithmic assertion. ◻
]

Only approximate length equations will be needed. To make their bit complexity explicit, suppose the data are known within $tau \/ 4$ and apply the displayed right inverse to those approximations. Approximate its output and $D p_i$, then round to rational positions $X_i$, with total Euclidean error at most $tau \/ 4$ per site. Defining $s_i = X_i - D p_i$ absorbs this rounding even though the $p_i$ may be irrational. Every contact equation then has error at most $tau \/ 4 + 2 \( tau \/ 4 \) < tau$, and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.12)")
 $ max_i \| s_i \| lt.eq 6 m #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] max_(cal(C)) \| d_(i j) \| + tau #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + tau . $ <eq:rounded-displacement>]

The number of arithmetic operations is polynomial, and all accumulated coefficients have polynomial size. Computing $sqrt(3)$ and the other real inputs to sufficiently many additional polynomially bounded bits therefore gives the stated absolute accuracy in polynomial time. In particular this does not call for an ill-conditioned general linear-system solve.

For later use, actual distances satisfy the exact vector identities and the elementary estimates

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.13)")
 $ X_j - X_i & = D u_(i j) + s_j - s_i &  & \( { i \, j } in cal(C) \) \,\
\| X_j - X_i \| & = D + u_(i j) dot.op \( s_j - s_i \) + O \( S^2 \/ D \) &  & \( { i \, j } in cal(C) \) \,\
\| X_j - X_i \| & gt.eq sqrt(2) D - 2 S &  & \( { i \, j } in.not cal(C) \) \, $ <eq:displaced-geometric-bounds>]

provided $max_i \| s_i \| lt.eq S$ and $S lt.eq D \/ 4$. The middle estimate is the Taylor bound for the Euclidean norm away from zero. The last is the triangle inequality and Lemma 5. No exact nonlinear prescribed-length problem is being solved; the quadratic remainder will be included in the hopping estimates.

=== 2.4 The half-filled Hubbard model <the-half-filled-hubbard-model>

We now realize those positive bonds as second-order hopping processes. Virtual double occupancy gives the antiferromagnetic superexchange mechanism of Anderson #cite(label("Anderson1959")). In our Pauli-matrix normalization the coefficient is $t^2 \/ U$ multiplying $h_(i j)^s - I$. We calculate the fermionic signs below and retain the constant term, because it contributes to the final decision thresholds.

Associate two fermionic modes, labelled $arrow.t \, arrow.b$, to each of the $m$ sites and restrict to exactly $m$ fermions. Let $c_(i sigma)^dagger \, c_(i sigma)$ satisfy the canonical anticommutation relations and put $n_(i sigma) = c_(i sigma)^dagger c_(i sigma)$. For nonnegative hopping strengths define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.14)")
 $ H_(upright("Hub")) \( t \) & = - sum_({ i \, j } in cal(C)) sum_(sigma = arrow.t \, arrow.b) t_(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] c_(i sigma)^dagger c_(j sigma) + c_(j sigma)^dagger c_(i sigma) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + U sum_i n_(i arrow.t) n_(i arrow.b) \,\
U & = integral.double_(bb(R)^3 times bb(R)^3) frac(phi.alt \( y \)^2 phi.alt \( y' \)^2, \| y - y' \|) thin thin upright(d) y thin thin upright(d) y' \, #h(2em) phi.alt \( y \) = pi^(- 1 \/ 2) e^(- \| y \|) . $ <eq:hubbard-definition>]

The radial probability density of $phi.alt^2$ is $p \( r \) = 4 r^2 e^(- 2 r)$. Angular integration gives the kernel $1 \/ max \( r \, s \)$: for $r \, s > 0$,

$ 1 / 2 integral_(- 1)^1 frac(thin upright(d) u, sqrt(r^2 + s^2 - 2 r s u)) = frac(1, max \( r \, s \)) . $

Since $integral_0^r p \( s \) thin thin upright(d) s = 1 - e^(- 2 r) \( 1 + 2 r + 2 r^2 \)$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.15)")
 $ U & = 8 integral_0^oo r e^(- 2 r) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] 1 - e^(- 2 r) \( 1 + 2 r + 2 r^2 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] thin thin upright(d) r\
 & = 8 (1 / 4 - 1 / 16 - 1 / 16 - 3 / 64) = 5 / 8 . $ <eq:onsite-integral>]

Thus $U$ is a fixed positive rational constant.

Let $q_(cal(C)) = \| cal(C) \|$ and define the rational scales

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.16)")
 $ overline(R) = max { 1 \, q_(cal(C)) Delta } \, #h(2em) eta = frac(U gamma, 1024 overline(R)^3) \, #h(2em) alpha = eta^2 / U \, #h(2em) f = frac(alpha gamma, 128 max { 1 \, q_(cal(C)) }) . $ <eq:hubbard-scales>]

The ideal hoppings are $t_(i j)^(\( 0 \)) = eta sqrt(K_(i j))$. For each contact compute a rational $r_(i j)$ with $\| r_(i j) - t_(i j)^(\( 0 \)) \| lt.eq f$, and output

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.17)")
 $ t_(i j) = max { f \, r_(i j) } . $ <eq:hopping-floor>]

Polynomial-precision bisection on nonnegative square roots (at most two nested square roots here) suffices to compute $r_(i j)$; no exact comparison between irrational numbers is required. The maximum in (2.17) is a comparison of rationals. All final hoppings satisfy $1 \/ "poly" \( n \) lt.eq t_(i j) < 1$, including those with $K_(i j) = 0$.

#thm([Proposition 2.6.])[
  (Hubbard reduction with rational hoppings).  #emph[The construction above is deterministic and polynomial time. With 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.18)")
 $ C_s = C_0 + sum_({ i \, j } in cal(C)) K_(i j) = 3 k Delta + 3 / 2 sum_e v_e^2 + sum_({ i \, j } in cal(C)) K_(i j) \, $ <eq:finite-scalar-shift>]

 it satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.19)")
 $ lr(|E #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H_(upright("Hub")) \( t \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] - alpha #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] E \( H_(upright("in")) \) - C_s #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]|) lt.eq frac(3 alpha gamma, 64) . $ <eq:finite-combined-error>]

 The scalar $C_s$ has polynomial magnitude and can be approximated to any polynomial-bit absolute precision in polynomial time.]
] <prop:hubbard>

#proof[
  First use the ideal hoppings $t^(\( 0 \))$. Let $A_U = U sum_i n_(i arrow.t) n_(i arrow.b)$ and let $T$ be the hopping part of $H_(upright("Hub")) \( t^(\( 0 \)) \)$. At filling $m$, the kernel of $A_U$ is exactly the singly occupied subspace: having $m$ fermions, at most one at each of $m$ sites, forces precisely one per site. Identify this subspace with $m$ spins, and denote its projection by $P_(upright("so"))$. The complementary excitation energies are positive integer multiples of $U$. A single hop from $P_(upright("so"))$ creates one vacant site and one doubly occupied site and hence has energy exactly $U$. In particular $P_(upright("so")) T P_(upright("so")) = 0$.
  A second hop can return to single occupancy only by moving an electron from that doubly occupied site to that vacant site. Thus the two hops must use the same unordered bond; products from different bonds have zero compression, even when the bonds share a vertex. For completeness, consider one bond $i \, j$ of strength $t$, with local mode order $i arrow.t \, i arrow.b \, j arrow.t \, j arrow.b$. Write $\| D_i chevron.r = c_(i arrow.t)^dagger c_(i arrow.b)^dagger \| 0 chevron.r$ and similarly for $\| D_j chevron.r$. The bond hopping operator $T_(i j)$ obeys
  $ T_(i j) \| arrow.t \, arrow.b chevron.r = - t \( \| D_i chevron.r + \| D_j chevron.r \) \, #h(2em) T_(i j) \| arrow.b \, arrow.t chevron.r = t \( \| D_i chevron.r + \| D_j chevron.r \) . $
  It consequently annihilates all triplets and maps the singlet to $- sqrt(2) t \( \| D_i chevron.r + \| D_j chevron.r \)$, of squared norm $4 t^2$. In an ordering containing other sites between $i$ and $j$, those sites are singly occupied during this two-hop process; their fixed fermion parity changes transition phases but not the compressed product. Equivalently, permuting the intervening mode pairs gives the same calculation under a fermionic change of basis. It follows that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.20)")
   $ - P_(upright("so")) T_(i j) A_U^(- 1) T_(i j) P_(upright("so")) = - frac(4 t^2, U) P_(upright("singlet") \, i j) = t^2 / U \( h_(i j)^s - I \) . $ <eq:hubbard-exchange>]
  Here $P_(upright("singlet") \, i j) = \( I - h_(i j)^s \) \/ 4$.
  For each spin, $c_(i sigma)^dagger c_(j sigma) + c_(j sigma)^dagger c_(i sigma)$ has norm one, so
  $ lr(bar.v.double T bar.v.double) lt.eq 2 sum_(cal(C)) t_(i j)^(\( 0 \)) = 2 eta sum_(cal(C)) sqrt(K_(i j)) lt.eq 2 eta overline(R) . $
  The last inequality uses $K_(i j) lt.eq Delta$ and $Delta gt.eq 1$. Lemma 3, with $g = U$ and $epsilon.alt = 2 eta overline(R) \/ U < 1 \/ 2$, and (2.20) give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.21)")
   $ lr(|E #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H_(upright("Hub")) \( t^(\( 0 \)) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] - alpha (E \( H_(+) \) - sum_(cal(C)) K_(i j))|) lt.eq frac(16 eta^3 overline(R)^3, U^2) lt.eq frac(alpha gamma, 64) . $ <eq:ideal-hubbard-error>]
  If the complementary subspace is empty, as can happen for $m = 1$, there is no hopping and this assertion is exact.
  For the rational hoppings, nonnegativity of $t_(i j)^(\( 0 \))$ and the accuracy of $r_(i j)$ imply $\| max { f \, r_(i j) } - t_(i j)^(\( 0 \)) \| lt.eq f$. Therefore
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.22)")
   $ ∥H_(upright("Hub")) \( t \) - H_(upright("Hub")) \( t^(\( 0 \)) \)∥ lt.eq 2 q_(cal(C)) f lt.eq frac(alpha gamma, 64) . $ <eq:hopping-rounding-error>]
  Also $t_(i j)^(\( 0 \)) lt.eq eta overline(R) lt.eq U \/ 1024$ and $f lt.eq U \/ \( 128 dot.op 1024^2 \)$, which verify $f lt.eq t_(i j) < 1$. The floor $f$ is an inverse polynomial. This argument covers an ideal hopping much smaller than $f$ and an ideal hopping equal to zero; no coefficient lower bound has been used. If $cal(C)$ is empty, (2.22) is simply zero.
  Finally, Proposition 4 with (2.7) contributes at most another $alpha gamma \/ 64$. Adding the three errors proves (2.19). All sizes, magnitudes, and reciprocal precision budgets in the construction are polynomial in $n$. Each $K_(i j)$ is either $Delta$, a square root of the rational number $2 Delta \| J_e \|$, or zero. The displayed scalar shift is thus efficiently approximable to the asserted precision, completing the proof. ◻
]

== 3 Isolated wells and deterministic energy tuning <sec:wells>

This section supplies an exponentially accurate classical procedure for tuning the individual well energies. The procedure operates on one-electron operators only; it does not diagonalize any of the spin or Hubbard Hamiltonians from 2.

There are two separate tasks. First we must show that moving a secondary positive nucleus changes one isolated energy monotonically, with much weaker effects on the other sites. Then we must evaluate those energies accurately enough to implement the resulting contraction. The required accuracy is exponential in $D$: an inverse-polynomial energy error in these dilated units would grow after the final amplification. The analytic and arithmetic estimates below address that precision cost.

Fix centers $X_1 \, dots.h \, X_m in bb(R)^3$ satisfying

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.1)")
 $ \| X_i - X_j \| gt.eq D - 2 S quad \( i eq.not j \) \, #h(2em) S lt.eq D \/ 100 . $ <eq:wells-separation>]

Here $D$ is a sufficiently large integer and $m \/ D$ is smaller than a sufficiently small absolute constant. All constants in this section are uniform in the centers and the parameters $rho_i in \[ 1 \, 4 \]$. The stronger scale requirements used later imply these hypotheses. Computational statements additionally assume rational centers. Their bit lengths, rather than their numerical magnitudes, enter the complexity bounds.

Choose an integer $Z_(*)$ with $e^D \/ 2 lt.eq Z_(*) lt.eq 2 e^D$. Round the following two numbers to nearest integers, once and for all, before tuning:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.2)")
 $ Z_(*) q & = "round" #h(-0.16667em) (Z_(*) (1 - frac(500 m, D))) \,\
Z_(*) q' & = "round" #h(-0.16667em) (Z_(*) frac(1000 m, D)) . $ <eq:wells-charges>]

Both integers are positive for the scales considered here. In particular,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
 $ q & = 1 - frac(500 m, D) + O \( Z_(*)^(- 1) \) \,\
q' & = frac(1000 m, D) + O \( Z_(*)^(- 1) \) \, #h(2em) c m / D lt.eq q' lt.eq C m / D . $ <eq:wells-charge-bounds>]

The primary and secondary nuclei of site $i$ are at $X_i$ and $X_i + rho_i e_z$, respectively, in scaled coordinates. Write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
 $ r_i & = \| y - X_i \| \, #h(2em) V_i \( y \) = q / r_i + frac(q', \| y - X_i - rho_i e_z \|) \,\
h & = - 1 / 2 Delta - sum_(i = 1)^m V_i . $ <eq:wells-full-one-body>]

These are the one-electron operators obtained from physical coordinates by $y = Z_(*) x$ and division of energies by $Z_(*)^2$.

Fix a nondecreasing $C^2$ function $chi : \[ 0 \, oo \) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ that vanishes on $\[ 0 \, 1 \/ 16 \]$ and equals one on $\[ 1 \/ 8 \, oo \)$. It can have rational piecewise-polynomial data: integrate a normalized positive multiple of $\( t - 1 \/ 16 \)^2 \( 1 \/ 8 - t \)^2$ on the transition interval and extend constantly outside. Set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.5)")
 $ chi_i \( y \) = chi \( r_i \/ D \) \, #h(2em) W_i = sum_(k eq.not i) chi_k V_k \, #h(2em) h_i = - 1 / 2 Delta - V_i - W_i . $ <eq:wells-capped>]

The caps remove the other nuclear singularities from $h_i$ while retaining their fields near site $i$.

=== 3.1 Uniform ground-state estimates <uniform-ground-state-estimates>

We use the three-dimensional Sobolev bounds $parallel u parallel_6 lt.eq C parallel u parallel_(H^1)$ and $parallel u parallel_oo lt.eq C parallel u parallel_(H^2)$, together with their localized versions. We also use the translated Hardy inequality

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.6)")
 $ ∥frac(u, \| thin dot.op - a thin \|)∥_2 lt.eq 2 parallel nabla u parallel_2 #h(2em) \( a in bb(R)^3 \) . $ <eq:wells-hardy>]

Indeed, integrate $"div" \( \( y - a \) \/ \| y - a \|^2 \) = \| y - a \|^(- 2)$ against $\| u \|^2$ for a smooth compactly supported $u$. Integration by parts and Cauchy–Schwarz give

$ parallel u \/ \| thin dot.op - a thin \| parallel_2^2 lt.eq 2 parallel u \/ \| thin dot.op - a thin \| parallel_2 parallel nabla u parallel_2 . $

Approximation proves (3.6) on $H^1$. Thus Coulomb multiplication maps $H^1$ continuously into $L^2$, uniformly in the location of the pole.

#thm([Proposition 3.1.])[
  (Uniform isolated-well estimates).  #emph[The operators $h_i$ are self-adjoint on $H^2 \( bb(R)^3 \)$, with form domain $H^1 \( bb(R)^3 \)$. Each has a simple ground eigenvalue $lambda_i$ and a real, positive, normalized eigenfunction $psi_i$. There is a constant $g > 0$ such that the remaining spectrum is at least $lambda_i + g$. For $phi.alt_i \( y \) = pi^(- 1 \/ 2) e^(- r_i)$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.7)")
 $ \| lambda_i + 1 \/ 2 \| lt.eq C m / D \, #h(2em) parallel psi_i - phi.alt_i parallel_(H^2) lt.eq C m / D . $ <eq:wells-h2>]

 Moreover, for derivative orders $j = 0 \, 1 \, 2$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.8)")
 $ parallel D^j W_i parallel_oo lt.eq C m / D^(j + 1) \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq W_i lt.eq frac(100 m, D) . $ <eq:wells-field-bounds>]

 These assertions hold before tuning and uniformly throughout $\[ 1 \, 4 \]^m$.]
] <prop:isolated-well>

#proof[
  On the nonzero support of $chi_k$, $r_k gt.eq D \/ 16$. Since $rho_k lt.eq 4$,
  $ \| y - X_k - rho_k e_z \| gt.eq r_k - 4 gt.eq r_k \/ 2 $
  for large $D$. The secondary singularity is inside the region where the cap is identically zero. Differentiation through order two, including derivatives of the cap, gives $parallel D^j \( chi_k V_k \) parallel_oo lt.eq C D^(- j - 1)$. Summing proves the derivative bounds. For example, $q lt.eq 1$, $q' lt.eq 1$, and sufficiently large $D$ give a bound below $100 \/ D$ for each capped potential, proving the stated function bound.
  Put $H_i^0 = - Delta \/ 2 - r_i^(- 1)$ and $B_i = h_i - H_i^0$. Hardy’s inequality and (3.8) give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.9)")
   $ parallel B_i u parallel_2 lt.eq C m / D parallel u parallel_(H^1) . $ <eq:wells-perturbation>]
  For any potential in these single-well operators, Hardy and Fourier interpolation give, for each $a > 0$, a uniform bound $parallel V u parallel_2 lt.eq a parallel Delta u parallel_2 + C_a parallel u parallel_2$. The Kato–Rellich theorem for relatively bounded perturbations #cite(label("Teschl2009")) Theorem 6.4, p. 135 therefore gives domain $H^2$. The same estimates, with the Laplacian term absorbed, prove uniform graph-norm equivalence:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.10)")
   $ C^(- 1) parallel u parallel_(H^2) lt.eq parallel h_i u parallel_2 + C parallel u parallel_2 lt.eq C parallel u parallel_(H^2) . $ <eq:wells-graph-norm>]
  The corresponding statement holds for $H_i^0$. At form level a fixed positive shift of either operator is bounded above and below by constant multiples of $parallel u parallel_(H^1)^2$, giving the asserted form domains.
  The hydrogenic ground state and its isolation follow without an excited-state formula. Direct integration by parts gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.11)")
   $ chevron.l u \, \( H_i^0 + 1 \/ 2 \) u chevron.r = 1 / 2 ∥nabla u + frac(y - X_i, r_i) u∥_2^2 . $ <eq:wells-hydrogen-square>]
  Its zero vectors are exactly the multiples of $phi.alt_i$. If normalized vectors orthogonal to $phi.alt_i$ had energies tending to $- 1 \/ 2$, they would be bounded in $H^1$. The Coulomb form is compact under weak convergence of such a sequence: within radius $a$ of the pole its absolute value is bounded by $C a parallel u parallel_(H^1)^2$, by Sobolev and Hölder; beyond radius $R$ it is bounded by $R^(- 1) parallel u parallel_2^2$; on the intervening annulus local compactness applies. A weak limit would therefore obey
  $ chevron.l u \, H_i^0 u chevron.r lt.eq - 1 \/ 2 \, #h(2em) chevron.l u \, H_i^0 u chevron.r gt.eq - 1 / 2 parallel u parallel_2^2 gt.eq - 1 \/ 2 . $
  Equality forces norm one and a multiple of $phi.alt_i$, contrary to orthogonality. This proves a fixed gap $g_0 > 0$.
  By (3.9), after a fixed positive shift the perturbed form lies between $1 - C m \/ D$ and $1 + C m \/ D$ times the unperturbed shifted form. The variational principle puts its first level within $C m \/ D$ of $- 1 \/ 2$, and its second variational level at least $- 1 \/ 2 + g_0 - C m \/ D$. The negative first level is attained. The preceding truncation argument gives form compactness of both own Coulomb potentials and of the bounded decaying $W_i$. A normalized minimizing sequence is bounded in $H^1$. Passing to a weakly convergent subsequence with limit $u$, compactness and lower semicontinuity give
  $ lambda_i parallel u parallel_2^2 lt.eq chevron.l u \, h_i u chevron.r lt.eq lambda_i < 0 . $
  Since $parallel u parallel_2 lt.eq 1$, these inequalities force $parallel u parallel_2 = 1$ and attainment. For small enough $m \/ D$ the first level is simple and separated from the rest by a fixed $g > 0$. Taking the absolute value of a real minimizer cannot increase its energy. The Euler equation and the strong maximum principle on the connected complement of the poles make it positive there.
  The form bounds give $parallel psi_i parallel_(H^1) lt.eq C$. Its equation implies
  $ \( H_i^0 + 1 \/ 2 \) psi_i = \( lambda_i + 1 \/ 2 \) psi_i - B_i psi_i \, $
  with right side of norm at most $C m \/ D$. On $phi.alt_i^perp$, the inverse of $H_i^0 + 1 \/ 2$ is bounded from $L^2$ to $H^2$: use the gap for the $L^2$ bound, then (3.10). The orthogonal component of $psi_i$ consequently has $H^2$ norm $O \( m \/ D \)$. Normalization and the positive overlap with $phi.alt_i$ put its coefficient along $phi.alt_i$ within $O \( \( m \/ D \)^2 \)$ of one. This proves (3.7) and the uniform sup bound. Positivity also holds at the poles by continuity and this sup-norm approximation, since both poles lie within distance four of $X_i$. ◻
]

=== 3.2 Moving the secondary nucleus <moving-the-secondary-nucleus>

The derivative of a moving Coulomb potential is not an $L^2$-bounded multiplier. We instead control its expectations by Hardy’s inequality.

#thm([Lemma 3.2.])[
  (Bracketing and uniform secant bounds).  #emph[With centers and rounded charges fixed, for every choice of the other parameters, $lambda_i \( rho_i = 1 \) < - 1 \/ 2 < lambda_i \( rho_i = 4 \)$. For $1 lt.eq a < b lt.eq 4$, holding the other parameters fixed, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.12)")
 $ c q' \( b - a \) lt.eq lambda_i \( b \) - lambda_i \( a \) lt.eq C q' \( b - a \) . $ <eq:wells-own-slope>]

 If instead $rho_i$ is held fixed, then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.13)")
 $ \| lambda_i \( rho \) - lambda_i \( tilde(rho) \) \| lt.eq C q' m / D^2 parallel rho - tilde(rho) parallel_oo . $ <eq:wells-other-slope>]

]
] <lem:wells-secants>

#proof[
  Taking the inner product of the eigen-equation with $phi.alt_i$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.14)")
   $ lambda_i = - 1 / 2 + \( 1 - q \) - q' f \( rho_i \) - chevron.l phi.alt_i \, W_i phi.alt_i chevron.r + O \( \( m \/ D \)^2 \) \, $ <eq:wells-energy-expansion>]
  where
  $ f \( rho \) = integral_(bb(R)^3) frac(pi^(- 1) e^(- 2 \| y \|), \| y - rho e_z \|) thin upright(d) y . $
  Here $chevron.l phi.alt_i \, psi_i chevron.r = 1 + O \( \( m \/ D \)^2 \)$, and replacing $psi_i$ by $phi.alt_i$ in a perturbing expectation costs $O \( \( m \/ D \)^2 \)$, by (3.9) and (3.7). Also $chevron.l phi.alt_i \, r_i^(- 1) phi.alt_i chevron.r = 1$. The spherical average of $\| r omega - rho e_z \|^(- 1)$ is $1 \/ max \( r \, rho \)$, as direct integration in $omega dot.op e_z$ shows. Consequently,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.15)")
   $ f \( rho \) & = rho^(- 1) integral_0^rho 4 r^2 e^(- 2 r) thin upright(d) r + integral_rho^oo 4 r e^(- 2 r) thin upright(d) r \,\
f' \( rho \) & = - rho^(- 2) integral_0^rho 4 r^2 e^(- 2 r) thin upright(d) r . $ <eq:wells-radial-potential>]
  In particular,
  $ f \( 1 \) = 1 - 2 e^(- 2) > 0.7 \, quad f \( 4 \) lt.eq 1 / 4 \, quad frac(1 - 5 e^(- 2), 16) lt.eq - f' \( rho \) lt.eq 1 quad \( 1 lt.eq rho lt.eq 4 \) . $
  At the first endpoint the leading shift in (3.14) is at most $\( 500 - 700 \) m \/ D$; at the second it is at least $\( 500 - 250 - 100 \) m \/ D$. The quadratic and rounding errors fit inside these strict margins, proving bracketing.
  For the secant bound, fix a ground function $u$ at either endpoint, and put $A \( t \) = X_i + t e_z$. The kernel derivative is dominated by $\| y - A \( t \) \|^(- 2)$, and Hardy gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.16)")
   $ integral frac(\| u^2 - phi.alt_i^2 \|, \| y - A \( t \) \|^2) thin upright(d) y & lt.eq ∥frac(u - phi.alt_i, \| thin dot.op - A \( t \) thin \|)∥_2 ∥frac(u + phi.alt_i, \| thin dot.op - A \( t \) thin \|)∥_2\
 & lt.eq 4 parallel nabla \( u - phi.alt_i \) parallel_2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] parallel nabla u parallel_2 + parallel nabla phi.alt_i parallel_2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] lt.eq C m / D . $ <eq:wells-density-hardy>]
  The same bound with $u^2$ justifies absolute integration in $y$ and $t$. Thus replacing the density by $phi.alt_i^2$ when integrating the moving-pole kernel derivative incurs at most $C q' \( m \/ D \) \( b - a \)$ error. The variational principle gives
  $ chevron.l psi_i \( b \) \, \( h_i \( b \) - h_i \( a \) \) psi_i \( b \) chevron.r lt.eq lambda_i \( b \) - lambda_i \( a \) lt.eq chevron.l psi_i \( a \) \, \( h_i \( b \) - h_i \( a \) \) psi_i \( a \) chevron.r . $
  Both bounding quantities differ from $- q' \( f \( b \) - f \( a \) \)$ by the error just estimated, proving (3.12).
  For $k eq.not i$, the moving secondary pole in $chi_k V_k$ stays at least $D \/ 32$ from the region where its cap is nonzero. Its bounded potential changes in sup norm by at most $C q' D^(- 2) \| rho_k - tilde(rho)_k \|$. Summing and applying the variational principle for a bounded additive perturbation proves (3.13). ◻
]

=== 3.3 Uniform tails <uniform-tails>

#thm([Lemma 3.3.])[
  (Crude tails before tuning).  #emph[Uniformly for all $rho in \[ 1 \, 4 \]^m$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.17)")
 $ \| psi_i \( y \) \| lt.eq C exp \[ - .99 \( r_i - .01 D \) \] \, $ <eq:wells-tail>]

 and, for sufficiently large $D$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.18)")
 $ parallel psi_i parallel_(H^1 \( { r_i > .65 D } \)) lt.eq e^(- .61 D) . $ <eq:wells-h1-tail>]

]
] <lem:well-tail>

#proof[
  On $r_i gt.eq .01 D$, both own poles are far away and $V_i + W_i = O \( m \/ D \)$. For $a = .99$ and $b \( y \) = C exp \[ - a \( r_i - .01 D \) \]$, direct calculation gives
  $ frac(\( h_i - lambda_i \) b, b) = - a^2 / 2 + a / r_i - V_i - W_i - lambda_i . $
  Its constant part is $\( 1 - a^2 \) \/ 2 + O \( m \/ D \) > 0$ at the chosen scales. The zero-order coefficient of $h_i - lambda_i$ is also positive throughout this exterior region. The uniform sup bound fixes $C$ so that $b gt.eq \| psi_i \|$ at the inner boundary. An $H^2 \( bb(R)^3 \)$ function is uniformly continuous and vanishes at infinity; otherwise uniform continuity on a sequence of disjoint balls would contradict its square integrability. On a large finite annulus add a constant $epsilon > 0$ to $b$ to dominate both signs of $psi_i$ at the outer boundary. The supersolution inequality is preserved. Testing the negative part of $b + epsilon minus.plus psi_i$ proves comparison. Send the outer radius to infinity and then $epsilon$ to zero. Inside $.01 D$, the same sup bound proves (3.17).
  For the gradient estimate choose a smooth cutoff $zeta$ that vanishes on $r_i lt.eq .645 D$, equals one on $r_i gt.eq .65 D$, and has gradient $O \( D^(- 1) \)$. Testing the eigen-equation with $zeta^2 psi_i$ gives
  $ 1 / 2 parallel nabla \( zeta psi_i \) parallel_2^2 = integral \( V_i + W_i + lambda_i \) \| zeta psi_i \|^2 thin upright(d) y + 1 / 2 integral \| nabla zeta \|^2 \| psi_i \|^2 thin upright(d) y . $
  All coefficients on this support are bounded. Thus (3.17) bounds the exterior $H^1$ norm by a polynomial in $D$ times $exp \[ - .99 \( .645 - .01 \) D \]$. Since $.99 \( .645 - .01 \) = .62865 > .61$, the polynomial factor is absorbed for large $D$. ◻
]

=== 3.4 An analytic estimate with uniform constants <an-analytic-estimate-with-uniform-constants>

We give the local regularity argument used in the numerical procedure. It explains why the shrinking length scales near a moving Coulomb pole do not increase the required polynomial degree beyond $O \( D \)$.

#thm([Lemma 3.4.])[
  (Uniform analytic patches).  #emph[Suppose $u$ is bounded on the unit ball in $bb(R)^3$ and satisfies $Delta u = F u$ there, where 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.19)")
 $ parallel partial^beta F parallel_oo lt.eq A^(\| beta \| + 1) \| beta \| ! quad upright("for every multi-index ") beta . $ <eq:wells-analytic-coefficient>]

 There is a constant $A_1$, depending only on $A$, such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.20)")
 $ max_(\| alpha \| = k) parallel partial^alpha u parallel_(L^oo \( B_(1 \/ 4) \)) lt.eq parallel u parallel_(L^2 \( B_1 \)) A_1^(k + 1) k ! . $ <eq:wells-analytic-derivatives>]

 On a sufficiently small fixed ball about the origin, Taylor polynomials consequently have geometrically decreasing errors in both value and first derivative, with constants depending only on $A$.]
] <lem:wells-analytic>

#proof[
  For two concentric balls of radii between $1 \/ 2$ and $1$, separated by $h$, the local estimates needed below are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.21)")
   $ parallel D^2 v parallel_(2 \, upright("inner")) & lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] parallel Delta v parallel_(2 \, upright("outer")) + h^(- 2) parallel v parallel_(2 \, upright("outer")) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
parallel nabla v parallel_(2 \, upright("inner")) & lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] h parallel Delta v parallel_(2 \, upright("outer")) + h^(- 1) parallel v parallel_(2 \, upright("outer")) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:wells-local-h2>]
  To obtain them, insert smooth cutoffs with first and second derivatives $O \( h^(- 1) \)$ and $O \( h^(- 2) \)$. Integration by parts against a squared cutoff and Cauchy–Schwarz, with $a b lt.eq \( h a \)^2 \/ 2 + \( b \/ h \)^2 \/ 2$, bound first derivatives on an intermediate ball as in (3.21). Apply the whole-space Fourier inequality $parallel D^2 w parallel_2 lt.eq C parallel Delta w parallel_2$ to the cutoff function and substitute that first-derivative bound in its commutator terms to obtain (3.21). Approximation proves these estimates for weak solutions. Successive localized Laplacian regularity makes $u$ smooth when $F$ is smooth, justifying the following differentiations.
  Fix an integer $ell gt.eq 2$, set $r_j = 1 - j \/ \( 2 ell \)$, and write
  $ T_j = max_(\| alpha \| = j) parallel partial^alpha u parallel_(L^2 \( B_(r_j) \)) \, #h(2em) M = parallel u parallel_(L^2 \( B_1 \)) . $
  The first-derivative bound gives $T_1 lt.eq C \( ell + A \/ ell \) M$. For $j lt.eq ell - 2$, apply (3.21) to an order-$j$ derivative between $B_(r_j)$ and $B_(r_(j + 2))$. Leibniz’ rule yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.22)")
   $ T_(j + 2) lt.eq C (sum_(b = 0)^j binom(j, b) A^(b + 1) b ! thin T_(j - b) + ell^2 T_j) . $ <eq:wells-analytic-recurrence>]
  Lower-order derivatives are available on the larger balls $B_(r_(j - b))$. The multi-index coefficient sum of order $b$ is $binom(j, b)$, by the multinomial identity. If preceding orders satisfy $T_s lt.eq M \( K ell \)^s$, the sum is at most
  $ A M \( K ell \)^j sum_(b = 0)^j \( A \/ K \)^b lt.eq 2 A M \( K ell \)^j $
  for $K gt.eq 2 A$, using $binom(j, b) b ! lt.eq ell^b$. Increasing the fixed $K$ absorbs the remaining $C ell^2$ factor into $\( K ell \)^2$. Both parity chains therefore satisfy $T_j lt.eq M \( K ell \)^j$.
  Set $ell = k + 2$. Sobolev embedding on fixed smaller balls bounds the sup norm of an order-$k$ derivative on $B_(1 \/ 4)$ by $C M \[ K \( k + 2 \) \]^(k + 2)$. The inequality $k ! gt.eq \( k \/ e \)^k$, with the small cases absorbed in the constant, converts this into (3.20). Taylor’s formula, summed over multi-indices, bounds the degree-$L$ remainder on $\| z \| lt.eq a$ by a constant times $\( C_1 a \)^(L + 1)$. The first-derivative remainder has an additional factor polynomial in $L$ and the same geometric decay. Fixing $a$ small enough that $C_1 a < 1$ proves the last assertion. ◻
]

=== 3.5 A polynomial-size graded Ritz space <a-polynomial-size-graded-ritz-space>

Graded meshes with increasing polynomial degree are a standard way to combine singular local behavior with exponential approximation away from the singularities. For related eigenvalue approximation results with singular potentials, see Maday and Marcati #cite(label("MadayMarcati2019")). Their bounded-domain discontinuous-Galerkin estimates are not an oracle for the present moving attractive Coulomb wells. We construct the needed conforming space and then certify its matrix arithmetic separately. More specifically, the products of tensor-linear hats with local polynomials below form a partition-of-unity approximation space, as in Melenk and Babuška #cite(label("MelenkBabuska1996")) Section 2. We give the cutoff and derivative estimates explicitly because the tensor cells can have arbitrarily large aspect ratios.

This is a form-domain approximation: the piecewise polynomial functions need only belong to $H^1$. Their kinetic matrix uses first weak derivatives, not second derivatives across element boundaries.

#thm([Lemma 3.5.])[
  (Exponentially accurate Ritz approximation).  #emph[For each $i$, each rational choice of centers and secondary parameters, and each sufficiently large $D$, one can explicitly construct a space $cal(V)_i subset H^1 \( bb(R)^3 \)$ of rational piecewise polynomials, of dimension polynomial in $D$, supported in $r_i < .8 D$, such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.23)")
 $ inf_(v in cal(V)_i) parallel v - psi_i parallel_(H^1) lt.eq e^(- .58 D) . $ <eq:wells-ritz-h1>]

 Its lowest Ritz value $lambda_i^(upright(R))$ satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.24)")
 $ 0 lt.eq lambda_i^(upright(R)) - lambda_i lt.eq C e^(- 1.16 D) . $ <eq:wells-ritz-bias>]

 The construction and constants are uniform over rational parameters throughout $\[ 1 \, 4 \]^m$.]
] <lem:wells-ritz-approximation>

#proof[
  Work in local coordinates $z = y - X_i$ on $\[ - D \, D \]^3$, with poles at $0$ and $rho_i e_z$. Put $delta = 2^(- 8 D)$ and fix a sufficiently small rational $xi > 0$, as specified below. On each coordinate axis let $B$ be the set of projections of the two poles. Starting from $\[ - D \, D \]$, bisect dyadically until every interval $I$ obeys
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.25)")
   $ \| I \| lt.eq xi min { 1 \, "dist" \( I \, B \) + delta } . $ <eq:wells-grid-rule>]
  Every leaf length is at least $xi delta \/ 2$, since its parent failed the stopping rule. Above a fixed spacing there are $O \( D \)$ intervals. At a finer level of length $h$, only intervals within $h \/ xi$ of one of the at most two projections can still split, so a fixed number of intervals per level are split there. There are $O \( log \( D \/ delta \) \) = O \( D + log D \)$ levels, hence $O \( D \)$ intervals per axis. This remains true for poles arbitrarily close to dyadic boundaries. The tensor product has $O \( D^3 \)$ nodes and cells.
  Let $b_w$ be the tensor-linear hat at node $w$. The full hats form a partition of unity in the cube. Retain nodes satisfying
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.26)")
   $ \| w \| < .73 D \, #h(2em) \| w \| > delta \, #h(2em) \| w - rho_i e_z \| > delta \, $ <eq:wells-retained-nodes>]
  and take $b_w \( z \) \( z - w \)^alpha$ for all $\| alpha \| lt.eq L_0$, where $L_0$ is a sufficiently large fixed multiple of $D$. These functions span $cal(V)_i$, whose dimension before removing dependencies is $O \( D^6 \)$.
  Here is the support estimate that avoids any aspect-ratio assumption. For either pole $a$, every coordinate interval adjacent to $w$ obeys
  $ \| I \| lt.eq xi min { 1 \, \| w_j - a_j \| + delta } . $
  The support radius $R_w^(upright("hat"))$ of a retained hat thus satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.27)")
   $ R_w^(upright("hat")) & lt.eq xi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| w - a \| + sqrt(3) delta #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] lt.eq \( 1 + sqrt(3) \) xi \| w - a \| \,\
R_w^(upright("hat")) & lt.eq sqrt(3) xi . $ <eq:wells-patch-radius>]
  For $d_w = min { 1 \, \| w \| \, \| w - rho_i e_z \| }$ this gives $R_w^(upright("hat")) lt.eq C xi d_w$. Each support avoids both poles and, for large $D$, lies in $\| z \| < .8 D$.
  Choose a fixed $c < 1 \/ 10$ and set $R = c d_w$. The whole ball $B \( w \, R \)$ avoids both poles. Throughout it every remote primary has distance at least $D - 2 S - .73 D - c > D \/ 8$, so it also avoids every remote cap transition. On this enlarged ball the eigen-equation contains only pure Coulomb potentials. Rescaling by $R$ gives $Delta u = F u$ on the unit ball. Its coefficient satisfies (3.19) with an absolute $A$: local inverse-distance expansions give factorial derivative bounds because $R \/ "dist" \( w \, a \) lt.eq c$; the extra $R^2$ in the coefficient makes $R^2 \/ "dist" \( w \, a \)$ bounded; and remote contributions sum to at most $C m \/ D$. The eigenvalue term is bounded too. The sup norm of $u$ is uniform by 8.
  Choose $xi$ sufficiently small compared to $c$ and the fixed analytic radius in 11, putting the hat supports inside that smaller ball after rescaling. With $L_0$ a sufficiently large fixed multiple of $D$, its Taylor polynomial $p_w$ satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.28)")
   $ \| psi_i - p_w \| + R \| nabla psi_i - nabla p_w \| lt.eq e^(- 20 D) quad upright("on ") "supp" b_w . $ <eq:wells-taylor-error>]
  Increasing the fixed multiple absorbs the polynomial factor in the differentiated remainder. The Taylor coefficients establish existence of an approximant; the algorithm will not compute them.
  To remove tiny neighborhoods of the poles and the exterior, let $a_(upright("out"))$ equal one up to radius $.66 D$, decrease linearly to zero by $.72 D$, and vanish thereafter. Let $a_(upright("in"))$ vanish up to $delta$, increase linearly to one by $2 delta$, and equal one thereafter. Sample
  $ a_(upright("out")) \( \| z \| \) thin a_(upright("in")) \( \| z \| \) thin a_(upright("in")) \( \| z - rho_i e_z \| \) $
  at all grid nodes to get weights $c_w in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$, and put $b = sum_w c_w b_w$. Nonzero weights occur only at retained nodes. The function $b$ equals one away from $O \( delta \)$-balls about the poles and away from $\| z \| > .65 D$: a node within $2 delta$ of a pole has adjacent lengths $O \( xi delta \)$, whereas all intervals have length at most $xi$. These facts localize the inner and outer transitions, respectively.
  Each first derivative of a tensor-linear interpolant is a convex combination of edge secants in that coordinate. Consequently $\| nabla b \| lt.eq C \/ delta$ on the inner transitions and $\| nabla b \| lt.eq C \/ D$ on the outer transition, irrespective of aspect ratios. The bounded sup norm of $psi_i$ makes the inner cutoff-gradient cost $C delta^(1 \/ 2)$. Also, by its $H^2$ bound,
  $ parallel nabla psi_i parallel_(L^2 \( B_(C delta) \( a \) \)) lt.eq C delta parallel nabla psi_i parallel_6 lt.eq C delta . $
  The exterior cost follows from (3.18). Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.29)")
   $ parallel psi_i - b psi_i parallel_(H^1) lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] sqrt(delta) + e^(- .61 D) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:wells-cutoff-error>]
  The cutoff weights, like the Taylor coefficients, are only existence coefficients in the explicitly generated space.
  Set $v = sum_w c_w b_w p_w in cal(V)_i$. At almost every point at most eight hats are nonzero. Their derivatives cost at most $C \/ delta$, by the minimum interval length, and $R^(- 1) lt.eq C \/ delta$. On a region of volume $O \( D^3 \)$, (3.28) gives
  $ parallel b psi_i - v parallel_(H^1) lt.eq C D^(3 \/ 2) delta^(- 1) e^(- 20 D) . $
  Since $sqrt(delta) = e^(- 4 \( log 2 \) D)$ and $delta^(- 1) e^(- 20 D) = e^(- \( 20 - 8 log 2 \) D)$, this and (3.29) prove (3.23) for large $D$.
  Finally, the form of $h_i - lambda_i$ is bounded in absolute value by a constant times the $H^1$ norm squared. For $e = v - psi_i$, the eigen-equation cancels the cross terms, giving
  $ frac(chevron.l v \, h_i v chevron.r, parallel v parallel_2^2) - lambda_i = frac(chevron.l e \, \( h_i - lambda_i \) e chevron.r, parallel v parallel_2^2) lt.eq C parallel e parallel_(H^1)^2 . $
  Here $parallel v parallel_2$ is bounded away from zero. The variational principle supplies the opposite inequality for the lowest Ritz value, proving (3.24). ◻
]

=== 3.6 Certified polynomial-bit eigenvalue evaluation <certified-polynomial-bit-eigenvalue-evaluation>

A small Ritz space by itself does not establish bit complexity. We next bound the conditioning loss and give certified matrix entries and eigenvalue enclosures. All arithmetic below is classical.

#thm([Proposition 3.6.])[
  (Uniform single-well eigenvalue oracle).  #emph[Suppose the centers and the parameters in $\[ 1 \, 4 \]^m$ are rational, and all their numerators and denominators, as well as those of $q \, q'$, have at most $B$ bits. Under the hypotheses of this section, a deterministic algorithm returns rational numbers $ell_i lt.eq lambda_i lt.eq u_i$ with 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.30)")
 $ u_i - ell_i lt.eq epsilon_D \, #h(2em) epsilon_D = 2^(- ceil.l 8 D \/ 5 ceil.r) < e^(- 1.1 D) . $ <eq:wells-oracle-precision>]

 Its bit complexity is polynomial in $D \, m \, B$. The bound is uniform over every rational sample in $\[ 1 \, 4 \]^m$; no spectral gap of a Ritz matrix is assumed.]
] <prop:ritz-oracle>

#proof[
  We divide the construction into exact polynomial arithmetic, certified Coulomb integration, and rational eigenvalue enclosure.
  #emph[Exact matrices and a mass lower bound.] Construct the basis in 12. All grid decisions are rational comparisons: one-dimensional distances in (3.25) are rational, and node membership in (3.26) is checked by comparing squared distances. Grid depth is $O \( D + log D \)$. Endpoints, hat coefficients, and coefficients of the degree-$O \( D \)$ monomials have polynomial bit length. For instance, raising a rational coordinate of $b$ bits to degree $L$ costs at most $O \( L b \)$ bits, before polynomial sums; here $b = O \( B + D + log D \)$ and $L = O \( D \)$. Only polynomially many coefficients and rational operations occur in any polynomial expansion or box integral. Summing rational terms by a product of their denominators gives a conservative polynomial bit bound even without exploiting common denominators.
  Let $f_1 \, dots.h \, f_(r_0)$ be this possibly redundant basis, $r_0 = O \( D^6 \)$. The mass and kinetic matrices
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.31)")
   $ M_(a b) = integral f_a f_b thin upright(d) y \, #h(2em) T_(a b) = 1 / 2 integral nabla f_a dot.op nabla f_b thin upright(d) y $ <eq:wells-exact-matrices>]
  are exact rationals obtained by box integration. A product of two hats has only a fixed number of box pieces; alternatively, summing over the entire polynomial-size grid also suffices. Remove redundant functions by exact rational Gram-matrix elimination, retaining an independent subset. This gives a positive definite matrix $M$ of dimension $r lt.eq r_0$, with corresponding kinetic matrix $T$. The subset is nonempty by the approximation property.
  Let $B_0$ be the largest bit length of a numerator or positive denominator of the entries of this reduced $M$, enlarged to at least one. It is computed from the exact entries and is polynomial in $D \, m \, B$. If $Q$ is the product of all entry denominators, then $Q lt.eq 2^(r^2 B_0)$ and $Q M$ is an integer positive definite matrix. Therefore $det \( Q M \) gt.eq 1$, while $lambda_max \( M \) lt.eq r 2^(B_0)$. The explicitly computable number
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.32)")
   $ mu = 2^(- K_M) \, #h(2em) K_M = r^3 B_0 + \( r - 1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] B_0 + ceil.l log_2 r ceil.r #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $ <eq:wells-mass-lower>]
  satisfies $lambda_min \( M \) gt.eq mu$. This intentionally coarse determinant bound is enough: $K_M$ is polynomial. Exact rank selection is itself polynomial-bit arithmetic. After clearing denominators, fraction-free elimination has entries given by minors of an integer matrix. Hadamard’s determinant bound bounds their bit lengths by a polynomial in its dimension and entry bit length. Equivalently, ordinary rational elimination with fractions reduced at each step has the same polynomial bound through the bordered-minor formulas.
  #emph[Certified Coulomb entries.] The preceding determinant bound shows how many extra bits compensate for a poorly conditioned basis. It remains to compute each potential entry to that absolute accuracy. The graded supports avoid every pole, which permits a uniformly convergent kernel expansion on each box. On the supports of the basis functions every other cap equals one, so every potential entry is a finite sum of pure Coulomb integrals against polynomials on rational boxes. For each such box $Q_0$, each pole $a$, and its midpoint $c_0$, put
  $ s = \| c_0 - a \|^2 \, #h(2em) z \( y \) = frac(2 \( c_0 - a \) dot.op \( y - c_0 \) + \| y - c_0 \|^2, s) . $
  Every contributing box is in the support of a retained hat. By (3.27), its diameter is a fixed small fraction of its distance from either own pole. Remote poles are farther still. Decreasing the fixed $xi$ if necessary gives $\| z \( y \) \| lt.eq 1 \/ 2$ throughout every such box. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.33)")
   $ frac(1, \| y - a \|) = s^(- 1 \/ 2) sum_(j = 0)^J binom(- 1 \/ 2, j) z \( y \)^j + cal(R)_J \( y \) \, #h(2em) \| cal(R)_J \( y \) \| lt.eq s^(- 1 \/ 2) 2^(- J) . $ <eq:wells-coulomb-series>]
  The coefficient bound $lr(|binom(- 1 \/ 2, j)|) = 4^(- j) binom(2 j, j) lt.eq 1$ proves this geometric remainder bound by summing the tail.
  Here $s$ and all coefficients of $z$ are exact rationals. Both $log_2^(+) \( s^(- 1 \/ 2) \)$ and the bit lengths of these rationals are polynomial in $D \, m \, B$; indeed the own-pole distances on supports are bounded below by a fixed multiple of $delta$. The positive scalar $s^(- 1 \/ 2)$ can be approximated to any requested polynomial number of bits by rational bisection and exact squaring.
  We spell out an absolute-error certificate, so cancellation among polynomial terms causes no difficulty. If $P$ is the polynomial multiplying a kernel on $Q_0$, a rational coefficient-and-volume bound gives
  $ A_(Q_0) gt.eq integral_(Q_0) \| P \( y \) \| thin upright(d) y . $
  One may take the volume times the sum of absolute coefficients, each multiplied by the appropriate coordinate bound to its degree. Its bit length is polynomial. Include the charge in this bound. If $s^(- 1 \/ 2) lt.eq 2^b$, the truncation error is at most $A_(Q_0) 2^(b - J)$. An approximation to $s^(- 1 \/ 2)$ of absolute error $2^(- q_0)$ adds at most $2 A_(Q_0) 2^(- q_0)$, since the absolute sum in (3.33) is at most two. For $T_0$ box–pole summands, taking
  $ J & gt.eq p + b + ceil.l log_2^(+) A_(Q_0) ceil.r + ceil.l log_2 T_0 ceil.r + 3 \,\
q_0 & gt.eq p + ceil.l log_2^(+) A_(Q_0) ceil.r + ceil.l log_2 T_0 ceil.r + 4 $
  makes their total error smaller than $2^(- p)$. Use maxima over the summands to choose common orders. Here $log_2^(+) t = max { 0 \, log_2 t }$; integer upper bounds can be read directly from rational numerator and denominator bit lengths.
  The polynomial $z \( y \)^j$ has degree $2 j$ in three variables. Even after multiplication by the basis polynomials it has only $O \( \( D + J + 1 \)^3 \)$ possible monomials. Polynomial multiplication, integration, and coefficient bit lengths are polynomial in $D \, m \, B \, J \, q_0$. These bounds establish a deterministic entry algorithm with any prescribed polynomial number $p$ of absolute precision bits. No matrix element is computed by integrating across a Coulomb singularity or a cap transition.
  #emph[Matrix error and exact eigenvalue decisions.] Let $A$ be the exact form matrix of $h_i$ in the independent basis. Compute a symmetric rational matrix $tilde(A)$ by evaluating each potential entry, once per unordered index pair, to error at most
  $ tau = frac(epsilon_D mu, 16 r) . $
  The kinetic matrix is exact. Since a symmetric matrix’s operator norm is at most $r$ times its largest absolute entry,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.34)")
   $ parallel M^(- 1 \/ 2) \( tilde(A) - A \) M^(- 1 \/ 2) parallel lt.eq epsilon_D \/ 16 . $ <eq:wells-relative-matrix-error>]
  The required entry precision has $O \( D + K_M + log r \)$ bits and is therefore polynomial. Let $tilde(lambda)$ be the minimum of the generalized Rayleigh quotient for $\( tilde(A) \, M \)$. The variational characterization and (3.34) give
  $ \| tilde(lambda) - lambda_i^(upright(R)) \| lt.eq epsilon_D \/ 16 . $
  An initial rational bracket is $\[ - R_0 \, R_0 \]$, where
  $ R_0 = 1 + frac(r max_(a \, b) \| tilde(A)_(a b) \|, mu) . $
  Its bit length is polynomial. To bisect it at a rational $z$, test the inertia of $tilde(A) - z M$ exactly. For clarity, an elementary exact inertia algorithm repeatedly uses congruence and Schur complements. A nonzero diagonal entry is a one-dimensional pivot and contributes its sign. If every remaining diagonal vanishes but an off-diagonal entry $a eq.not 0$ remains, pivot on the block $mat(delim: "(", 0, a; a, 0)$, which contributes one positive and one negative direction. A zero remaining matrix contributes only zero directions. These operations give the full inertia. After denominators are cleared, each Schur entry is a ratio of bordered minors; determinant bounds again give polynomial bit growth. Thus this test is a polynomial-time exact rational calculation, including the case of zero eigenvalues.
  Because $M$ is positive definite, $tilde(A) - z M$ is positive semidefinite exactly when $z lt.eq tilde(lambda)$. Update the lower or upper endpoint accordingly. Polynomially many bisections give a rational enclosure $\[ ell \, u \]$ of $tilde(lambda)$ of width at most $epsilon_D \/ 8$. Arbitrary proximity to a bisection point incurs no further precision requirement and no eigenvalue-gap assumption.
  The fixed lower bound on $D$ can be enlarged so that $C e^(- 1.16 D) lt.eq epsilon_D \/ 8$; this is possible since $\( 8 \/ 5 \) log 2 < 1.16$. Combining the one-sided Ritz bias and the two-sided matrix error gives the certified interval
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.35)")
   $ lambda_i in [ell - epsilon_D / 16 - epsilon_D / 8 \, u + epsilon_D / 16] . $ <eq:wells-certified-enclosure>]
  Its width is at most $3 epsilon_D \/ 8$. Notice that the Ritz bias is subtracted from the lower endpoint; a Ritz upper bound alone would not suffice for tuning. This proves the proposition.
  All dimensions, operation counts, and working bit lengths are polynomial in $D \, m \, B$. The mesh and the independent subset may change at different rational samples, but every such sample obeys the same estimates. Constants controlling the polynomial degree, the cutoff size, and the minimum admissible $D$ are fixed once from the uniform inequalities above. They are not input-dependent advice or a search for an unknown spectral gap. ◻
]

=== 3.7 Simultaneous rational tuning <simultaneous-rational-tuning>

The energy oracle now supplies certified intervals, while 9 supplies the monotonicity and weak coupling of the tuning parameters. Combining these facts gives a rational algorithm: each coordinate is bracketed separately, and simultaneous updates contract. The stopping rule below uses an interval containing the target energy; it never asks for the exact sign of an unknown eigenvalue difference.

#thm([Proposition 3.7.])[
  (Tuning with fixed rounded charges).  #emph[At any fixed rational centers satisfying the hypotheses of this section, and with the integer charges in (3.2) already fixed, one can compute rational $rho_1 \, dots.h \, rho_m in \[ 1 \, 4 \]$ such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.36)")
 $ \| lambda_i + 1 \/ 2 \| lt.eq e^(- 1.01 D) #h(2em) \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq m \) . $ <eq:wells-tuned>]

 The algorithm is deterministic and polynomial in $D \, m$ and the input bit lengths. Every parameter it samples or outputs has $O \( D \)$ fractional bits.]
] <prop:tuning>

#proof[
  For frozen parameters other than $rho_i$, the endpoint brackets, continuity, and strict secant bound in 9 give a unique root of $lambda_i = - 1 \/ 2$ in $\[ 1 \, 4 \]$. Let $T_i \( rho \)$ be that root and let $T = \( T_1 \, dots.h \, T_m \)$, using the old other coordinates in every update. Combining (3.12) and (3.13) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.37)")
   $ parallel T \( rho \) - T \( tilde(rho) \) parallel_oo lt.eq theta parallel rho - tilde(rho) parallel_oo \, #h(2em) theta = C m \/ D^2 lt.eq 1 \/ 2 . $ <eq:wells-contraction>]
  Thus $T$ has a unique fixed point $rho^(*)$ in the cube. This also follows directly by iterating the map: its successive differences are geometrically summable, and the limit is fixed by continuity.
  We implement each coordinate update with a certified scalar bisection. Start with $\[ 1 \, 4 \]$. At its midpoint invoke 13. If the whole resulting eigenvalue interval lies below $- 1 \/ 2$, retain the upper half of the parameter interval; if it lies above $- 1 \/ 2$, retain the lower half. If it contains $- 1 \/ 2$, stop and return that sample. In this last case the true energy differs from $- 1 \/ 2$ by at most $epsilon_D$, so (3.12) puts the sample within $C epsilon_D \/ q'$ of the exact root. Otherwise, after $J = ceil.l 4 D ceil.r$ bisections return the midpoint of the remaining parameter interval. All sign decisions were certified, so that interval still contains the root and has length $3 thin 2^(- J)$. Since $q' lt.eq 1$ and $2^(- 4 D) lt.double epsilon_D$, the same update-error bound holds:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.38)")
   $ parallel hat(T) \( rho \) - T \( rho \) parallel_oo lt.eq C epsilon_D \/ q' . $ <eq:wells-update-error>]
  This comparison-or-stop rule never requests an exact sign of an unknown irrational eigenvalue at a putative zero.
  Start with all coordinates equal to two and perform $J$ approximate simultaneous updates. If $e_j$ is the sup-norm distance to $rho^(*)$, then
  $ e_(j + 1) lt.eq 1 / 2 e_j + C epsilon_D \/ q' \, #h(2em) e_J lt.eq 3 thin 2^(- J) + 2 C epsilon_D \/ q' . $
  The own-coordinate upper slope and the other-coordinate variation bound together give
  $ \| lambda_i \( rho \) - lambda_i \( rho^(*) \) \| lt.eq C q' parallel rho - rho^(*) parallel_oo . $
  Consequently the output has
  $ \| lambda_i + 1 \/ 2 \| lt.eq C q' 2^(- J) + C epsilon_D lt.eq e^(- 1.01 D) $
  for large enough $D$, since $\( 8 \/ 5 \) log 2 > 1.1 > 1.01$.
  Each scalar bisection restarts from the rational interval $\[ 1 \, 4 \]$ and makes at most $O \( D \)$ midpoint operations. Its samples, including early-return samples, therefore have $O \( D \)$ fractional bits independently of the number of simultaneous iterations. There are $O \( m D^2 \)$ oracle calls. Their data bit lengths consist of the fixed-center and rounded-charge bit lengths together with these $O \( D \)$ parameter bits, so 13 proves the total polynomial-time bound. In particular the charge-rounding error is part of every evaluated operator and is compensated by tuning; it is not introduced as an uncontrolled perturbation afterward. ◻
]

== 4 Relative tails and computable hopping calibration <sec:hopping>

The absolute orbital estimate of Proposition 8 does not determine an overlap of order $e^(- D)$. This section proves a relative tail estimate, identifies positive integrals that determine the hoppings, and shows how to calibrate those integrals before tuning the single-well energies. All constants below are independent of the site layout, $D$, the displacements, and the parameters $rho_i in \[ 1 \, 4 \]$. The cap profile $chi$ is fixed throughout.

We use the positions and operators from Section 3. In particular,

$ X_i = D p_i + s_i \, #h(2em) max_i \| s_i \| lt.eq S \, #h(2em) \| X_i - X_j \| gt.eq D - 2 S quad \( i eq.not j \) . $

We take $D$ sufficiently large and, for the estimates below, may assume $m + S lt.eq D^(1 \/ 4)$. Where a relative error is expanded, its displayed bound is assumed smaller than a fixed positive constant. The final parameter choice in Section 6 satisfies these conditions and makes every such error as small as required.

=== 4.1 Relative comparison of single-well tails <relative-comparison-of-single-well-tails>

For $r_i = \| y - X_i \|$ define the ray primitive

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ F_i \( y \) = r_i integral_0^1 W_i #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] X_i + t \( y - X_i \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] thin thin upright(d) t \, #h(2em) F_i \( X_i \) = 0 . $ <eq:ray-primitive>]

The integral follows the entire segment from $X_i$ to $y$; it is not a local approximation to the field at either endpoint. Write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ epsilon_(upright("rel")) = frac(m log D + m^2, D) . $ <eq:relative-tail-error>]

#thm([Proposition 4.1.])[
  (Uniform relative tail).  #emph[Suppose the actual rounded charges and the parameters $rho_i$ obey the conclusions of Proposition 14, in particular $\| lambda_i + 1 \/ 2 \| lt.eq e^(- 1.01 D)$. Uniformly for $r_i lt.eq 2 D$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
 $ psi_i \( y \) = pi^(- 1 \/ 2) e^(- r_i + F_i \( y \)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + O \( epsilon_(upright("rel")) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:relative-tail>]

]
] <prop:relative-tail>

#proof[
  Fix a site, suppress its subscript, and write $r = \| y - X \|$ and $omega = \( y - X \) \/ r$ when $r > 0$. The capped-field estimates in Proposition 8 give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
   $ parallel W parallel_oo lt.eq frac(C m, D) \, #h(2em) parallel nabla W parallel_oo lt.eq frac(C m, D^2) \, #h(2em) parallel D^2 W parallel_oo lt.eq frac(C m, D^3) . $ <eq:hopping-field-derivatives>]
  Set $A \( y \) = integral_0^1 W \( X + t \( y - X \) \) thin thin upright(d) t$, so $F = r A$. For $0 < r lt.eq 3 D$, differentiation gives
  $ nabla F & = omega A + r integral_0^1 t nabla W \( X + t \( y - X \) \) thin thin upright(d) t \,\
Delta F & = frac(2 A, r) + 2 omega dot.op integral_0^1 t nabla W \( X + t \( y - X \) \) thin thin upright(d) t\
 & quad + r integral_0^1 t^2 Delta W \( X + t \( y - X \) \) thin thin upright(d) t . $
  Consequently,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
   $ \| nabla F \| lt.eq frac(C m, D) \, #h(2em) \| Delta F \| lt.eq C (frac(m, D r) + m / D^2) . $ <eq:ray-derivative-bounds>]
  Also $F \( r \, omega \) = integral_0^r W \( X + t omega \) thin thin upright(d) t$, and hence $partial_r F = W \( y \)$ exactly.
  Let $f = pi^(- 1 \/ 2) e^(- r + F)$. A direct calculation on the annulus $10 lt.eq r lt.eq 3 D$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.6)")
   $ frac(\( h_i - lambda_i \) f, f) & = - \( lambda_i + 1 \/ 2 \) + 1 / r - V_i - 1 / 2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] Delta F + \| nabla F \|^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
lr(|frac(\( h_i - lambda_i \) f, f)|) & lt.eq C (frac(m, D r) + m^2 / D^2) . $ <eq:relative-tail-residual>]
  In the first equality the term $partial_r F$ cancels $W_i$. For the second, note that $rho_i lt.eq 4$ and $r gt.eq 10$, so
  $ lr(|1 / r - V_i \( y \)|) lt.eq frac(\| 1 - q \|, r) + frac(q', r - 4) lt.eq frac(C m, D r) . $
  The charge-rounding errors and $e^(- 1.01 D)$ are absorbed by the right-hand side of (4.6).
  Choose a sufficiently large fixed constant $A_0$ and put
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.7)")
   $ g \( r \) = A_0 [m / D #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + log \( r \/ 10 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + frac(m^2 r, D^2)] . $ <eq:tail-barrier-correction>]
  Then
  $ g' = A_0 (frac(m, D r) + m^2 / D^2) \, #h(2em) Delta g = A_0 (frac(m, D r^2) + frac(2 m^2, D^2 r)) \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq Delta g lt.eq frac(2 g', r) . $
  Replacing $f$ by $f e^(plus.minus g)$ adds to its operator quotient the term
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.8)")
   $ plus.minus \( 1 - partial_r F \) g' minus.plus 1 / 2 Delta g - 1 / 2 \( g' \)^2 . $ <eq:barrier-signs>]
  Since $r gt.eq 10$, $partial_r F = O \( m \/ D \)$, and $g'$ is uniformly small for sufficiently large $D$, the added term is at least $c g'$ for the plus sign and at most $- c g'$ for the minus sign. Increasing $A_0$ dominates (4.6). Thus $f e^g$ is a supersolution and $f e^(- g)$ a subsolution.
  At $r = 10$, Proposition 8 and Sobolev embedding give $psi_i = pi^(- 1 \/ 2) e^(- 10) + O \( m \/ D \)$, while $F = O \( m \/ D \)$. The reference value $e^(- 10)$ is a fixed positive number. A further fixed increase of $A_0$ therefore ensures $f e^(- g) lt.eq psi_i lt.eq f e^g$ on the inner boundary. On the outer boundary $r = 3 D$, Lemma 10 gives
  $ \| psi_i \| lt.eq C e^(- 0.99 \( 3 - 0.01 \) D) = C e^(- 2.9601 D) . $
  Moreover $0 lt.eq F lt.eq C m$ there. With $kappa = e^(- 2.5 D)$, the functions
  $ f_(+) = f e^g + kappa \, #h(2em) f_(-) = f e^(- g) - kappa $
  satisfy $f_(-) lt.eq psi_i lt.eq f_(+)$ on both boundary spheres for large $D$. On the annulus the zero-order coefficient obeys
  $ - V_i - W_i - lambda_i gt.eq 1 / 2 - 1 / 10 - O \( m \/ D \) gt.eq 1 / 4 . $
  Adding the positive constant $kappa$ preserves the supersolution inequality, and subtracting it preserves the subsolution inequality. The maximum principle therefore yields the same inequalities throughout the annulus. For completeness, its weak form here follows by testing a supersolution-minus-solution with its nonnegative negative part: the resulting identity is the negative of a sum of a gradient norm and a strictly positive weighted $L^2$ norm, so the negative part vanishes. The same argument applies to a solution-minus-subsolution.
  On $10 lt.eq r lt.eq 2 D$, one has $g \( r \) = O \( epsilon_(upright("rel")) \)$ and, since $F gt.eq 0$,
  $ kappa / f lt.eq sqrt(pi) thin e^(- D \/ 2) . $
  The comparison inequalities imply (4.3) on this region. On $r lt.eq 10$, the reference exponential is bounded below by a fixed positive constant, $F = O \( m \/ D \)$, and the uniform absolute orbital estimate gives the same relative conclusion directly. ◻
]

=== 4.2 Surrogate fields and positive hopping integrals <surrogate-fields-and-positive-hopping-integrals>

We first identify the attraction integral to be calibrated. Write $V_k^(upright("hole")) = \( 1 - chi_k \) V_k$ for each site $k$, and for an ordered pair of distinct sites $\( i \, j \)$ define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.9)")
 $ B_(i j) = chevron.l psi_j \, V_j^(upright("hole")) psi_i chevron.r . $ <eq:oriented-hole-integral>]

The identity

$ h = h_i - sum_(k eq.not i) V_k^(upright("hole")) $

shows that the hole at $j$ contributes $- B_(i j)$ to the matrix element $chevron.l psi_j \, \( h + 1 \/ 2 \) psi_i chevron.r$ in the nonorthogonal orbitals. Section 5 will bound its remaining terms and the change under orthonormalization.

The relative tail formula still contains the secondary parameters, which have not yet been computed when the primary centers must be placed. We replace its capped field by an explicit one depending only on the primary centers. The replacement error is relative and uniform in the entire tuning cube; this is what makes the construction order possible.

Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.10)")
 $ W_i^(*) \( y \) = sum_(k eq.not i) frac(chi \( \| y - X_k \| \/ D \), \| y - X_k \|) \, #h(2em) F_i^(*) \( y \) = r_i integral_0^1 W_i^(*) \( X_i + t \( y - X_i \) \) thin thin upright(d) t . $ <eq:surrogate-field>]

Each summand is extended by zero through its cutoff hole. In particular the apparent quotient at $y = X_k$ is identically zero in a neighborhood of that point.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  (Uniform capped kernels and surrogate replacement).  #emph[The zero-extended kernels 
$ G_D \( z \) = frac(chi \( \| z \| \/ D \), \| z \|) \, #h(2em) J_(D \, rho) \( z \) = frac(chi \( \| z \| \/ D \), \| z - rho e_z \|) $

 are globally $C^2$, uniformly for $1 lt.eq rho lt.eq 4$. For every multi-index $alpha$ with $\| alpha \| lt.eq 2$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.11)")
 $ parallel partial^alpha G_D parallel_oo + parallel partial^alpha J_(D \, rho) parallel_oo lt.eq C D^(- 1 - \| alpha \|) . $ <eq:capped-kernel-bounds>]

 Uniformly over all allowed secondary positions and rounded charges, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.12)")
 $ parallel W_i - W_i^(*) parallel_oo lt.eq frac(C m^2, D^2) \, #h(2em) \| F_i \( y \) - F_i^(*) \( y \) \| lt.eq frac(C m^2, D) quad \( r_i lt.eq 3 D \) . $ <eq:surrogate-error>]

 Consequently, for tuned wells the relative formula (4.3) holds with $F_i^(*)$ in place of $F_i$ and the same error bound $O \( epsilon_(upright("rel")) \)$.]
] <lem:surrogate>

#proof[
  For large $D$, both poles are strictly inside $\| z \| < D \/ 16$, where the kernels vanish. On the nonzero part of either kernel, $\| z \| gt.eq D \/ 16$ and $\| z - rho e_z \| gt.eq \| z \| \/ 2$. Rescale by $z = D zeta$. Both kernels are $D^(- 1)$ times a function whose derivatives through order two are uniformly bounded: the shifted pole in the second rescaled kernel has position $\( rho \/ D \) e_z$, a fixed positive distance from the support of $chi$. The $C^2$ matching of $chi$ at its transition endpoints makes these global bounds, including at the hole boundaries. This proves (4.11).
  Since $\| q - 1 \| + q' lt.eq C m \/ D$ after rounding,
  $ W_i - W_i^(*) = sum_(k eq.not i) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] \( q - 1 \) G_D \( y - X_k \) + q' J_(D \, rho_k) \( y - X_k \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] $
  has sup norm $O \( m^2 \/ D^2 \)$. Integration over a ray of length at most $3 D$ proves the second bound in (4.12). Exponentiating this bound in Proposition 15 proves the final assertion. None of the kernel estimates require tuning; they hold uniformly on the whole parameter cube. ◻
]

For an oriented contact $\( i \, j \)$, replacing the two orbital factors in $B_(i j)$ by their relative approximations and the local nuclear potential by the unit primary kernel leads to the explicit positive integral

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.13)")
 $ T_(i j) \( X \) & = 1 / pi integral_(\| v \| lt.eq D \/ 8) frac(1 - chi \( \| v \| \/ D \), \| v \|) thin e^(L_X \( v \)) thin thin upright(d)^3 v \,\
L_X \( v \) & = - \| X_j + v - X_i \| - \| v \| + F_i^(*) \( X_j + v \) + F_j^(*) \( X_j + v \) . $ <eq:positive-hopping-integral>]

The fields in this definition use all centers in $X$. The integrable singularity $\| v \|^(- 1)$ is part of the nonnegative integration weight, not part of the exponential. The next lemma justifies these replacements.

#thm([Lemma 4.3.])[
  (Comparison with the uncapped attraction).  #emph[For tuned wells and every contact, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.14)")
 $ B_(i j) = T_(i j) \( X \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + O \( epsilon_(upright("rel")) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:hole-surrogate-comparison>]

 The constants are uniform in $rho_j in \[ 1 \, 4 \]$.]
] <lem:positive-hopping>

#proof[
  On the support of $V_j^(upright("hole"))$, write $y = X_j + v$. Then $\| v \| lt.eq D \/ 8$ and $\| y - X_i \| lt.eq D + 2 S + D \/ 8 < 2 D$. Proposition 15 and Lemma 16 apply to both wave functions. Their product is $pi^(- 1) e^(L_X \( v \))$ times a uniform $1 + O \( epsilon_(upright("rel")) \)$ factor. The primary kernel has coefficient $q = 1 + O \( m \/ D \)$, so it remains to compare the secondary kernel with the primary one in the resulting positive integral.
  For $\| v \| > 10$,
  $ frac(\| v \|, \| v - rho_j e_z \|) lt.eq frac(\| v \|, \| v \| - 4) lt.eq 5 / 3 . $
  For $\| v \| lt.eq 10$, the hole weight is one when $D$ is large. The ray derivative estimates, also valid for $F^(*)$, show that $L_X$ is Lipschitz on this ball with a fixed constant. This statement requires no differentiability of $\| v \|$ at zero; it follows either by integrating the bounded derivatives away from zero or directly from the ray formula. Thus $e^(L_X \( v \))$ is within fixed factors of $e^(L_X \( 0 \))$ throughout the ball. Moreover,
  $ integral_(\| v \| lt.eq 10) frac(thin upright(d)^3 v, \| v - rho_j e_z \|) lt.eq integral_(\| w \| lt.eq 14) frac(thin upright(d)^3 w, \| w \|) < oo \, #h(2em) integral_(\| v \| lt.eq 1) frac(thin upright(d)^3 v, \| v \|) > 0 . $
  The secondary-kernel integral on the inner ball is therefore at most a fixed multiple of the primary-kernel integral. Combining the two regions and multiplying by $q' = O \( m \/ D \)$ gives a relative $O \( m \/ D \)$ correction. Positivity allows the uniform relative wave-function errors to be taken outside both integrals. These errors are included in (4.14). ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 4.4.])[
  (Size of the reference integrals).  #emph[Let $X^0 = \( D p_i \)_i$. For every contact, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.15)")
 $ c e^(- D) lt.eq T_(i j) \( X^0 \) lt.eq C D^2 e^(- D + C m) . $ <eq:reference-hopping-bounds>]

]
] <lem:reference-hopping>

#proof[
  Put $u = u_(i j) = p_j - p_i$, so $\| u \| = 1$. The triangle inequality gives $\| D u + v \| + \| v \| gt.eq D$. Each ray correction in the integral is nonnegative and at most $C m$, by (4.11) and its ray length. The integral of $\| v \|^(- 1)$ over the ball of radius $D \/ 8$ is $O \( D^2 \)$, proving the upper bound. On $\| v \| lt.eq 1$ the hole weight is one, $\| D u + v \| + \| v \| lt.eq D + 2$, and the corrections are nonnegative. Integration over this fixed ball gives the lower bound; for example $2 e^(- D - 2)$ is a valid lower bound for large $D$. ◻
]

=== 4.3 Sensitivity to displacement <sensitivity-to-displacement>

#thm([Lemma 4.5.])[
  (Uniform displacement law).  #emph[Let $X_i = D p_i + s_i$ with $\| s_i \| lt.eq S$. There is a constant $c_1 > 0$ such that, uniformly on all contacts, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.16)")
 $ frac(T_(i j) \( X \), T_(i j) \( X^0 \)) & = e^(- u_(i j) dot.op \( s_j - s_i \)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + O \( epsilon_(upright("disp")) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
epsilon_(upright("disp")) & = frac(m S, D) + S^2 / D + S D^(- 1 \/ 4) + e^(- c_1 sqrt(D)) . $ <eq:hopping-displacement-law>]

 In particular the coefficient of the projected displacement in the leading exponent is one.]
] <lem:hopping-displacement>

#proof[
  Compare the two integrals at the same $v$. In the ray defining $F_i^(*) \( X_j + v \)$, the reference path point $D p_i + t \( D p_j + v - D p_i \)$ moves by $\( 1 - t \) s_i + t s_j$, of norm at most $S$. Each field center moves by at most $S$ as well. The globally Lipschitz bound in (4.11) therefore changes the field along the path by at most $C m S \/ D^2$. The ray has length $O \( D \)$ and its length changes by at most $2 S$. Since the field itself is $O \( m \/ D \)$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.17)")
   $ \| F_i^(*) \( X_j + v \; X \) - F_i^(*) \( D p_j + v \; X^0 \) \| lt.eq frac(C m S, D) . $ <eq:ray-displacement>]
  The same bound holds for the $F_j^(*)$ term; its ray length $\| v \|$ does not change. The global nature of the capped-kernel bound is essential here: it also applies when a moving ray crosses a hole or a transition interface.
  Write $u = u_(i j)$, $delta = s_j - s_i$, and $v = z u + w$ with $w perp u$. Since $\| v \| lt.eq D \/ 8$, $\| D u + v \| gt.eq 7 D \/ 8$. The Hessian of the norm on the segment from $D u + v$ to $D u + v + delta$ has norm $O \( 1 \/ D \)$. Its gradient at $D u + v$ differs from $u$ by $O \( \| w \| \/ D \)$. Taylor’s formula gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.18)")
   $ \| D u + v + delta \| - \| D u + v \| = u dot.op delta + O (frac(S \| w \|, D) + S^2 / D) . $ <eq:distance-displacement>]
  This estimate includes the whole backward segment $v = - t u$, $0 lt.eq t lt.eq D \/ 8$: there $D u + v = \( D - t \) u$ and the gradient is exactly $u$. Thus the segment’s non-negligible longitudinal extent creates neither a different coefficient nor an $O \( S \)$ transverse error.
  On the region $\| w \| lt.eq D^(3 \/ 4)$, Equations (4.17) and (4.18) show that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.19)")
   $ L_X \( v \) - L_(X^0) \( v \) + u dot.op delta = O (frac(m S, D) + S^2 / D + S D^(- 1 \/ 4)) . $ <eq:main-region-log-error>]
  The nonnegative integration weight is identical in the two integrals, so exponentiation controls their contributions on this region by the same relative factor.
  On the remaining region $\| w \| > D^(3 \/ 4)$, one has
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.20)")
   $ \| D u + v \| + \| v \| - D & gt.eq z + \| v \| = frac(\| w \|^2, \| v \| - z)\
 & gt.eq frac(4 \| w \|^2, D) gt.eq 4 sqrt(D) . $ <eq:transverse-excess>]
  The identity is used only for $w eq.not 0$, and the denominator is at most $2 \| v \| lt.eq D \/ 4$. The field corrections are bounded by $C m$, without any angular constancy assumption. Displacement changes a distance by at most $2 S$, and $\| u dot.op delta \| lt.eq 2 S$. It follows that the omitted reference and displaced contributions, after division by $e^(- D)$ and $e^(- D - u dot.op delta)$ respectively, are at most
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.21)")
   $ C D^2 exp #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] - c sqrt(D) + C \( m + S \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] . $ <eq:transverse-integral-error>]
  By (4.15), these are also bounds on their contributions relative to the reference integral, with the appropriate displacement factor. The assumption $m + S lt.eq D^(1 \/ 4)$ and sufficiently large $D$ absorb both the possibly large anisotropic factor $e^(C m)$ and the polynomial volume factor into $e^(- c_1 sqrt(D))$. Combining this with (4.19), and using smallness of its right-hand side, proves the lemma. ◻
]

=== 4.4 Deterministic relative quadrature <deterministic-relative-quadrature>

We have reduced hopping calibration to positive integrals whose size is exponentially small. Direct absolute quadrature at that scale would obscure the complexity bound. Instead we control the oscillation of the logarithm of the exponential factor, while integrating its nonnegative radial weight exactly. Positivity then converts local errors into a relative error for the whole integral.

#thm([Lemma 4.6.])[
  (Computing the positive reference integrals).  #emph[For $0 < epsilon.alt < 1 \/ 10$, one can compute a positive rational $hat(I)_(i j)$ satisfying 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.22)")
 $ lr(|log frac(hat(I)_(i j), e^D T_(i j) \( X^0 \))|) lt.eq epsilon.alt $ <eq:reference-quadrature-accuracy>]

 by a deterministic algorithm polynomial in $D$, $m$, the bit lengths of the layout data, and $epsilon.alt^(- 1)$. In particular the algorithm is polynomial in the source size when $D$ is a fixed polynomial and $epsilon.alt$ an inverse polynomial.]
] <lem:hopping-quadrature>

#proof[
  Let $L = L_(X^0)$ in (4.13). On the integration ball, the two distance terms have Lipschitz constants one, and the ray formulas and (4.11) give a Lipschitz constant $C m \/ D$ for each correction. Hence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.23)")
   $ \| L \( v \) - L \( v' \) \| lt.eq C \| v - v' \| \, #h(2em) \| L \( v \) \| lt.eq C \( D + m \) . $ <eq:hopping-log-regularity>]
  Only the exponential factor has been included in this logarithm. No lower bound on $1 - chi$ or differentiability of $log \( 1 - chi \)$ is needed.
  We describe a mesh with exact positive weights. Parameterize the unit sphere, up to a set of area zero, by
  $ omega \( mu \, phi \) = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] sqrt(1 - mu^2) cos phi \, sqrt(1 - mu^2) sin phi \, mu #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Here $- 1 lt.eq mu lt.eq 1$, $0 lt.eq phi lt.eq 2 pi$, and $thin upright(d) omega = thin upright(d) mu thin thin upright(d) phi$. Use $M$ equal intervals in $mu$, $N$ equal intervals in $phi$, and a rational radial partition with mesh at most $h$. Insert all the finitely many breakpoints of $chi \( r \/ D \)$ into that partition. On a radial interval $I_a$, the weight
  $ w_a = integral_(I_a) \( 1 - chi \( r \/ D \) \) r thin thin upright(d) r $
  is a nonnegative exact rational, computable by polynomial integration. Discard intervals with zero weight. The normalized angular weight of every angular rectangle is exactly
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.24)")
   $ 1 / pi thin 2 / M thin frac(2 pi, N) = frac(4, M N) . $ <eq:exact-angular-weight>]
  Choose one sample $v_(a b c)$ in each resulting spatial cell, for example at the parameter midpoints.
  The square-root parameterization satisfies
  $ \| omega \( mu \, phi \) - omega \( mu' \, phi' \) \| lt.eq C \| mu - mu' \|^(1 \/ 2) + C \| phi - phi' \| . $
  Therefore $M = O \( \( D \/ h \)^2 \)$ and $N = O \( D \/ h \)$, with sufficiently large fixed constants, make every spatial cell have diameter $O \( h \)$, including cells adjacent to the poles. There are $O \( \( D \/ h \)^4 \)$ cells, a polynomial number. Their exact weighted sample sum is
  $ Q = sum_(a \, b \, c) frac(4 w_a, M N) thin e^(L \( v_(a b c) \)) . $
  Positivity and (4.23) imply
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.25)")
   $ e^(- C h) Q lt.eq T_(i j) \( X^0 \) lt.eq e^(C h) Q . $ <eq:positive-quadrature-bracket>]
  Thus a fixed multiple of $epsilon.alt$ is sufficient for $h$. This relative estimate is independent of the size $e^(- D)$ of the integral.
  It remains to specify evaluation and bit accuracy. For a ray of length $r lt.eq 2 D$, the integrand $t mapsto W_i^(*) \( X_i + t \( y - X_i \) \)$ has Lipschitz constant at most $C m r \/ D^2$. A midpoint rule with $K$ equal panels therefore approximates its ray primitive with error at most
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.26)")
   $ C frac(m r^2, D^2 K) lt.eq frac(C m, K) . $ <eq:ray-quadrature-error>]
  Taking $K = O \( m \/ epsilon.alt \)$ and evaluating each field sample to absolute error $O \( epsilon.alt \/ D \)$ gives an $O \( epsilon.alt \)$ error in each primitive. Each field sample is a sum of $m - 1$ globally Lipschitz capped kernels. At small radius the kernel is evaluated as zero, and elsewhere its denominator is bounded below by a fixed multiple of $D$; equivalently one evaluates the fixed continuous function $chi \( a \) \/ a$, extended by zero on $\[ 0 \, 1 \/ 16 \]$, at $a = \| z \| \/ D$. Its global Lipschitz bound controls errors even if the approximate radius lies on the other side of a transition breakpoint. No crossing location needs to be found.
  The coordinates $D p_i$, sample coordinates, square roots, and trigonometric values can be computed to inverse-polynomial absolute accuracy with polynomially many bits. The global kernel and ray bounds just proved control the resulting errors. Thus all sample logarithms can be approximated to an arbitrarily specified $O \( epsilon.alt \)$ absolute error with polynomial work. Such logarithm errors change the positive sample sum by a factor $e^(O \( epsilon.alt \))$, not by an additive error multiplied by an uncontrolled exponential.
  For the remaining arithmetic, compute the scaled sum $e^D Q$, using sample exponentials $e^(D + L \( v_(a b c) \))$. Its exact value is bounded below by a positive constant up to the factor in (4.25), because (4.15) gives $e^D T_(i j) \( X^0 \) gt.eq 2 e^(- 2)$. The sum of its non-exponential weights is $O \( D^2 \)$, and all exponent arguments have magnitude $O \( D + m \)$. After the logarithms have been approximated as above, each elementary exponential can be rounded to sufficiently small absolute error $O \( epsilon.alt \/ D^2 \)$, clipping a negative rounded value to zero; this adds $O \( epsilon.alt \)$ to the scaled sum. These last approximations require only $O \( D + m + log \( D \/ epsilon.alt \) \)$ precision bits, with polynomial working precision. This step does not require exponentially accurate ray quadrature: the earlier ray errors have already been bounded multiplicatively. Standard range reduction and Taylor series compute the square roots, trigonometric values, exponentials, and logarithms to these precisions in polynomial time. The exact rational weights have polynomial bit lengths, and the sum contains polynomially many terms.
  Allocate a sufficiently small fixed fraction of $epsilon.alt$ to each of the spatial, ray, coordinate, and arithmetic errors. The resulting positive rational sum is $hat(I)_(i j)$ and satisfies (4.22). ◻
]

=== 4.5 Calibration, rounding, and orientation <calibration-rounding-and-orientation>

The oriented attraction integrals are close to a symmetric matrix for an exact reason that is useful independently of their asymptotic formula.

#thm([Lemma 4.7.])[
  (Orientation identity).  #emph[For distinct sites, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.27)")
 $ B_(i j) - B_(j i) = \( lambda_i - lambda_j \) chevron.l psi_j \, psi_i chevron.r . $ <eq:hopping-orientation-identity>]

 For tuned wells and large $D$ under the stated separation bounds, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.28)")
 $ \| chevron.l psi_j \, psi_i chevron.r \| lt.eq e^(- 0.85 D) \, #h(2em) \| B_(i j) - B_(j i) \| lt.eq 2 e^(- 1.86 D) . $ <eq:hopping-orientation-error>]

]
] <lem:hopping-orientation>

#proof[
  The single-well operators have common domain $H^2$, and
  $ h_i - h_j = V_j^(upright("hole")) - V_i^(upright("hole")) . $
  Pair this identity between the real eigenfunctions $psi_j$ and $psi_i$, using self-adjointness of $h_j$, to obtain (4.27). All the pairings are well-defined by the Coulomb domain bounds of Proposition 8. Equivalently, in the two expansions of the full matrix element $chevron.l psi_j \, \( h + 1 \/ 2 \) psi_i chevron.r$, contributions from every hole other than $i$ and $j$ cancel identically, because they integrate the same real product against the same multiplication potential.
  To record an explicit overlap bound, Lemma 10 gives
  $ \| psi_i \( y \) psi_j \( y \) \| lt.eq C e^(0.0198 D) e^(- 0.99 \( r_i + r_j \)) . $
  Use $r_i + r_j gt.eq D - 2 S$ on a factor with exponent $0.90$, and retain $e^(- 0.09 r_i)$ for integrability. It follows that
  $ \| chevron.l psi_j \, psi_i chevron.r \| lt.eq C e^(- 0.8802 D + 1.8 S) integral_(bb(R)^3) e^(- 0.09 \| y - X_i \|) thin thin upright(d)^3 y lt.eq e^(- 0.85 D) $
  for sufficiently large $D$. Finally $\| lambda_i - lambda_j \| lt.eq 2 e^(- 1.01 D)$ proves the second bound. ◻
]

For a tolerance $tau > 0$, define the total calibration error

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.29)")
 $ cal(E)_(upright("cal")) =  & frac(m log D + m^2, D) + frac(m S, D) + S^2 / D\
 & + S D^(- 1 \/ 4) + tau + e^(- c_1 sqrt(D)) . $ <eq:total-calibration-error>]

#thm([Proposition 4.8.])[
  (Deterministic hopping calibration).  #emph[Let the contact layout be as in Lemma 5, choose one orientation per contact, and let the prescribed rational hoppings satisfy $0 < t_min lt.eq t_(i j) lt.eq 1$, where $t_min$ is an inverse polynomial in the source size. Let $Z_(*)$ be an integer with $e^D \/ 2 lt.eq Z_(*) lt.eq 2 e^D$ and round the nuclear charges as in Section 3. For inverse-polynomial $tau$, there is a deterministic polynomial-time procedure that computes rational centers $X_i = D p_i + s_i$ obeying 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.30)")
 $ S := max_i \| s_i \| lt.eq C m #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] m + log D + log \( 1 \/ t_min \) + 1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + tau . $ <eq:calibrated-displacement-size>]

 After applying Proposition 14 at these final centers, the resulting orbitals satisfy, on every chosen oriented contact, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.31)")
 $ \| Z_(*) B_(i j) - t_(i j) \| lt.eq C t_(i j) cal(E)_(upright("cal")) . $ <eq:calibrated-contact-error>]

 Here $D$ is taken sufficiently large for the hypotheses above and for $cal(E)_(upright("cal"))$ to be small. The reverse oriented attraction has an additional error at most $4 e^(- 0.86 D)$ in the same rescaled units. No additional geometric equation for the reverse orientation is needed.]
] <prop:calibration>

#proof[
  Compute, to additive error at most $tau \/ 4$, the quantities
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.32)")
   $ d_(i j) = log (frac(Z_(*) T_(i j) \( X^0 \), t_(i j))) \, #h(2em) X^0 = \( D p_i \)_i . $ <eq:calibration-increments>]
  Lemma 20 supplies the requisite relative integral accuracy. One can evaluate the logarithm as
  $ log Z_(*) - D + log #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] e^D T_(i j) \( X^0 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] - log t_(i j) \, $
  so all elementary-function calculations have polynomial bit cost. The two-sided bound (4.15) yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.33)")
   $ max_(\( i \, j \) in cal(C)) \| d_(i j) \| lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] m + log D + log \( 1 \/ t_min \) + 1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:calibration-increment-bound>]
  Apply the constructive linear right inverse in Lemma 6 to the approximated data. Its operator bound is $C m$ from the maximum edge datum to the maximum site displacement. Its formulas use rational operations and fixed elements of $bb(Q) \( sqrt(3) \)$, so they can be evaluated with polynomially many bits. Round the resulting positions $D p_i + s_i$ to rational positions with Euclidean site error at most $tau \/ 8$, allocating another such allowance if approximate arithmetic is used in the right inverse. Define the final displacements from these rational positions by $s_i = X_i - D p_i$. This definition preserves the exact contact vector decomposition even though $s_i$ need not be rational. Since every $u_(i j)$ is a unit vector, the resulting equations satisfy
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.34)")
   $ \| u_(i j) dot.op \( s_j - s_i \) - d_(i j) \| lt.eq tau . $ <eq:rounded-displacement-equations>]
  The right-inverse bound and (4.33) imply (4.30). For example, the $sqrt(3)$ arithmetic may be done to absolute accuracy $O \( tau \/ \[ D + "poly" \( m \) max \( 1 \, max \| d_(i j) \| \) \] \)$; the required number of bits is polynomial. Noncontacts retain separation at least $sqrt(2) D - 2 S$ by the triangle inequality.
  There is no requirement to realize prescribed distances exactly. For a contact with $delta = s_j - s_i$ the actual distance is
  $ \| D u_(i j) + delta \| = D + u_(i j) dot.op delta + O \( S^2 \/ D \) . $
  Actual coordinates automatically satisfy all vector-cycle identities; the displayed per-edge correction is already included in Lemma 19 and is not accumulated through a further nonlinear solve.
  Put $e_(i j) = u_(i j) dot.op \( s_j - s_i \) - d_(i j)$. The key cancellation is the exact identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.35)")
   $ Z_(*) T_(i j) \( X^0 \) e^(- u_(i j) dot.op \( s_j - s_i \)) = t_(i j) e^(- e_(i j)) \, #h(2em) \| e_(i j) \| lt.eq tau . $ <eq:exact-calibration-identity>]
  Lemma 19 therefore gives $Z_(*) T_(i j) \( X \) = t_(i j) \( 1 + O \( epsilon_(upright("disp")) + tau \) \)$. After tuning, Lemma 17 adds only $O \( epsilon_(upright("rel")) \)$ relative error. This proves (4.31). In particular no factor $e^(C m)$ or $e^(\| d_(i j) \|)$ multiplies its absolute error, even for the smallest floored hopping. Since $t_(i j) lt.eq 1$, the absolute error per contact is at most $C cal(E)_(upright("cal"))$; summing hopping operators incurs only polynomial factors in the number of sites.
  All computations of $X$ used the surrogate fields $W_i^(*)$. They depend on the primary centers and on $D$, but not on any eigenfunction or on the secondary parameters $rho_i$. Thus the algorithm first fixes the rounded charges, computes and rounds these centers, and only then invokes Proposition 14 at the final rational centers. The surrogate-field replacement bounds hold uniformly over the tuning cube, and the relative orbital comparisons hold uniformly over all tuned outputs. Thus tuning at these fixed centers requires no recalibration and introduces no subsequent position-rounding error.
  Finally Lemma 21 and $Z_(*) lt.eq 2 e^D$ give $Z_(*) \| B_(i j) - B_(j i) \| lt.eq 4 e^(- 0.86 D)$. The common target energy $- 1 \/ 2$ is sufficient; exact equality of the $lambda_i$ is not required. The full one-body operator is Hermitian, so one may use either orientation for its off-diagonal entry after the negligible other-hole terms are accounted for in Section 5. ◻
]

If the contact set is empty, the procedure simply rounds $D p_i$ to rational centers at the stated site accuracy; there are no hopping equations to solve. In general (4.30) is $S lt.eq "poly" \( n \) \( 1 + log D \)$. The bounds needed for the global choice are precisely (4.29), together with $m + S lt.eq D^(1 \/ 4)$ and the exponentially small orientation error. All scale choices and the conversion to the final many-electron energy error are made in Section 6.

== 5 Excluding lower-energy continuum states <sec:continuum>

We now compare the spectral infimum of the full Coulomb operator with the half-filled Hubbard energy. In particular, the comparison below includes a lower bound on every state outside the selected orbital space.

The proof has three distinct outputs. We first identify the one-particle matrix in orthonormal site modes and prove a fixed positive gap on its entire orthogonal complement. We then bound the many-electron coupling to that complement and eliminate it by a quadratic-form square completion. Only after this all-state lower bound is available do we compare the compressed Coulomb interaction with the Hubbard onsite term. Keeping these steps separate prevents a variational upper bound from being used as a NO-instance lower bound.

We use the rational centers and tuned parameters constructed in Sections 3 and 4. Increasing the fixed polynomial choice of $D$ if necessary, we may assume throughout this section that

$ m + S lt.eq D^(1 \/ 4) \, #h(2em) D gt.eq D_0 \, $

where the constant $D_0$ can be chosen uniformly. The estimates from Propositions 8 and 14 and Lemma 10 that we will use are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.1)")
 $ parallel psi_i - phi.alt_i parallel_(H^2) & lt.eq C m \/ D \, & parallel psi_i parallel_(H^2) & lt.eq C \,\
\| lambda_i + 1 \/ 2 \| & lt.eq e^(- 1.01 D) \, & \| psi_i \( y \) \| & lt.eq C e^(- .99 \( r_i - .01 D \)) . $ <eq:continuum-orbital-input>]

There is also a constant $g_(upright(w)) > 0$, independent of the construction parameters, such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.2)")
 $ h_i - lambda_i gt.eq g_(upright(w)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] I - \| psi_i chevron.r chevron.l psi_i \| #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:continuum-well-gap>]

All operator inequalities involving an unbounded operator are understood as quadratic-form inequalities. By Lemma 5 and the displacement bound,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.3)")
 $ \| X_i - X_j \| gt.eq D - 2 S quad \( i eq.not j \) \, #h(2em) \| X_i - X_j \| gt.eq sqrt(2) D - 2 S quad \( { i \, j } in.not cal(C) \) . $ <eq:continuum-separations>]

The second inequality concerns distinct noncontact sites.

=== 5.1 The one-particle matrix <the-one-particle-matrix>

Recall the hole potentials from 4, and write

$ A = h + 1 / 2 \, #h(2em) V_i^(upright("hole")) = \( 1 - chi_i \) V_i \, #h(2em) epsilon.alt_i = lambda_i + 1 / 2 . $

For the chosen orientation $i arrow.r j$ of a contact, define

$ B_(i j) = chevron.l psi_j \, V_j^(upright("hole")) psi_i chevron.r \, #h(2em) epsilon.alt_(upright("hop")) = max_(i arrow.r j) \| Z_(*) B_(i j) - t_(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|] . $

The maximum is zero if there are no contacts. Proposition 22 gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.4)")
 $ epsilon.alt_(upright("hop")) lt.eq C cal(E)_(upright("cal")) \, $ <eq:continuum-calibration-interface>]

because all the target hoppings are at most one. Let $sans(T)$ be the real symmetric $m$-by-$m$ matrix with entry $- t_(i j)$ on contacts and zero elsewhere, including its diagonal.

Define $cal(U) : bb(C)^m arrow.r L^2 \( bb(R)^3 \)$ by $cal(U) e_i = psi_i$, and let $G = cal(U)^(*) cal(U)$ be its Gram matrix. The notation $cal(U)$ denotes this map; the scalar onsite repulsion remains $U = 5 \/ 8$.

#thm([Lemma 5.1.])[
  (Residuals and the orthonormalized matrix).  #emph[For the preceding parameters and sufficiently large $D$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.5)")
 $ parallel A psi_i parallel_2 lt.eq e^(- .75 D) \, #h(2em) parallel G - I parallel lt.eq e^(- .85 D) . $ <eq:continuum-residual-gram>]

 In particular $G$ is positive definite. The isometry $cal(J)_0 = cal(U) G^(- 1 \/ 2)$ has orthonormal columns $omega_i = cal(J)_0 e_i$, spanning the same spatial orbital space. Its matrix satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.6)")
 $ parallel Z_(*) cal(J)_0^(*) A cal(J)_0 - sans(T) parallel & lt.eq delta_1 \,\
delta_1 & := m epsilon.alt_(upright("hop")) + Z_(*) e^(- 1.01 D) + C m Z_(*) e^(- 1.1 D) + C m Z_(*) e^(- 1.6 D) . $ <eq:continuum-one-body-error>]

 If $P_0 = cal(J)_0 cal(J)_0^(*)$, then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.7)")
 $ parallel A P_0 parallel lt.eq C sqrt(m) thin e^(- .75 D) \, #h(2em) parallel omega_i - phi.alt_i parallel_(H^1) lt.eq C m \/ D . $ <eq:continuum-projected-residual>]

]
] <lem:one-body-matrix>

#proof[
  Since $h = h_i - sum_(k eq.not i) V_k^(upright("hole"))$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.8)")
   $ A psi_i = epsilon.alt_i psi_i - sum_(k eq.not i) V_k^(upright("hole")) psi_i . $ <eq:continuum-residual-identity>]
  The $k$-th hole is supported in $r_k lt.eq D \/ 8$. On it,
  $ r_i gt.eq 7 D \/ 8 - 2 S quad \( k eq.not i \) . $
  Both Coulomb poles of $V_k$ lie in this hole for large $D$. The charges $q \, q'$ are bounded, and direct radial integration, enlarging the integration ball by at most four for the secondary pole, gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.9)")
   $ parallel V_k^(upright("hole")) parallel_2 lt.eq C D^(1 \/ 2) \, #h(2em) parallel V_k^(upright("hole")) parallel_1 lt.eq C D^2 . $ <eq:continuum-hole-norms>]
  Consequently the sum in (5.8) has norm at most
  $ C m D^(1 \/ 2) exp \( - .85635 D + 1.98 S \) . $
  Together with the tuning error, this is at most $e^(- .75 D)$ for the stated regime and sufficiently large $D$.
  For $i eq.not j$, put $ell_(i j) = \| X_i - X_j \|$. The product of the two tail bounds is at most
  $ C exp #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] - .99 \( r_i + r_j \) + .0198 D #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Use $r_i + r_j gt.eq ell_(i j)$ on a factor with exponent $.90$; the remaining factor is integrable uniformly in the centers, since $integral e^(- .09 \( r_i + r_j \)) thin thin upright(d) y lt.eq integral e^(- .09 r_i) thin thin upright(d) y lt.eq C$. Thus
  $ \| G_(i j) \| lt.eq C e^(- .90 ell_(i j) + .0198 D) lt.eq C e^(- .8802 D + 1.8 S) . $
  The diagonal entries are one. The row-sum estimate and $m + S lt.eq D^(1 \/ 4)$ prove the second inequality in (5.5).
  Let $M = cal(U)^(*) A cal(U)$, which is real symmetric. We next estimate its entries before orthonormalization. If $k eq.not i \, j$, both $r_i$ and $r_j$ are at least $7 D \/ 8 - 2 S$ on the $k$-th hole. Equations (5.1) and (5.9) therefore imply
  $ sum_(k eq.not i \, j) \| chevron.l psi_j \, V_k^(upright("hole")) psi_i chevron.r \| lt.eq C m D^2 e^(- 1.7127 D + 3.96 S) . $
  The same estimate bounds $sum_(k eq.not i) chevron.l psi_i \, V_k^(upright("hole")) psi_i chevron.r$. For a noncontact pair, the remaining hole at $j$ is bounded using $r_i + r_j gt.eq sqrt(2) D - 2 S$, giving
  $ \| chevron.l psi_j \, V_j^(upright("hole")) psi_i chevron.r \| lt.eq C D^2 e^(- \( .99 sqrt(2) - .0198 \) D + 1.98 S) . $
  Here $.99 sqrt(2) - .0198 > 1.38$. All these bounds are smaller than $e^(- 1.1 D)$, after absorbing their polynomial factors and fixed constants. For $i eq.not j$, the term $epsilon.alt_i G_(j i)$ is at most $e^(- 1.86 D)$. It follows, adjusting a fixed constant, that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.10)")
   $ \| M_(i i) - epsilon.alt_i \| & lt.eq C e^(- 1.1 D) \,\
\| M_(j i) + B_(i j) \| & lt.eq C e^(- 1.1 D) &  & \( i arrow.r j upright(" a chosen contact") \) \,\
\| M_(i j) \| & lt.eq C e^(- 1.1 D) &  & \( i eq.not j \, med { i \, j } in.not cal(C) \) . $ <eq:continuum-raw-entries>]
  The exactly symmetric matrix $M$ requires only one of the two orientations of each contact. Its diagonal tuning contribution has norm at most $e^(- 1.01 D)$, not $m e^(- 1.01 D)$. Entrywise comparison with $sans(T)$ and a row-sum bound now give
  $ parallel Z_(*) M - sans(T) parallel lt.eq m epsilon.alt_(upright("hop")) + Z_(*) e^(- 1.01 D) + C m Z_(*) e^(- 1.1 D) . $
  For completeness, the residual estimate controls the entire matrix, without using a dimension-dependent bound on a many-body space:
  $ parallel A cal(U) parallel lt.eq sqrt(m) thin e^(- .75 D) \, #h(2em) parallel cal(U) parallel = parallel G parallel^(1 \/ 2) lt.eq 2 \, #h(2em) parallel M parallel lt.eq C sqrt(m) thin e^(- .75 D) . $
  Functional calculus on the positive matrix $G$ gives $parallel G^(- 1 \/ 2) - I parallel lt.eq C e^(- .85 D)$ and $parallel G^(- 1 \/ 2) parallel lt.eq 2$. Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.11)")
   $ parallel G^(- 1 \/ 2) M G^(- 1 \/ 2) - M parallel lt.eq C sqrt(m) thin e^(- 1.6 D) lt.eq C m e^(- 1.6 D) . $ <eq:continuum-gram-correction>]
  This orthonormalizes the matrix of the shifted operator $h + 1 \/ 2$. In the original nonorthogonal modes, $M = cal(U)^(*) h cal(U) + 1 / 2 G$. The shift is essential in this estimate: one must add $1 / 2 G$, rather than $1 / 2 I$, before orthonormalization. Equation (5.6) follows.
  The same residual estimate applied to $A cal(J)_0 = A cal(U) G^(- 1 \/ 2)$ proves the first part of (5.7). Finally, as a map into $H^1$, $cal(U)$ has norm at most $C sqrt(m)$, by (5.1). Thus
  $ parallel omega_i - psi_i parallel_(H^1) lt.eq C sqrt(m) thin e^(- .85 D) . $
  Combining this with $parallel psi_i - phi.alt_i parallel_(H^1) lt.eq C m \/ D$ gives the remaining assertion. ◻
]

We now include spin. Set

$ frak(h) = L^2 \( bb(R)^3 \; bb(C)^2 \) \, #h(2em) P = P_0 ⊗ I_2 \, #h(2em) cal(J) = cal(J)_0 ⊗ I_2 . $

Thus $P_0$ has rank $m$, whereas $P$ has rank $2 m$. The operator $A$ acts trivially on spin; we use the same symbol for this extension. Its residual and matrix bounds are unchanged.

=== 5.2 A gap on the entire one-particle complement <a-gap-on-the-entire-one-particle-complement>

Spatial localization separates the nuclei from the exterior, where the attraction is uniformly weak. We use the IMS product-rule identity (compare Simon #cite(label("Simon1983")) Lemma 3.1); its kinetic-error coefficient is $1 \/ 2$ for our normalization $- Delta \/ 2$. The identity is derived below. The additional point to verify is that a globally orthogonal function has only exponentially small components along the local ground states after localization.

#thm([Proposition 5.2.])[
  (Continuum complement gap).  #emph[There is a constant $g > 0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $D$, 
$ \( I - P \) A \( I - P \) gt.eq g \( I - P \) . $

 The inequality holds on the full one-particle form domain, including functions supported between the nuclei or at infinity.]
] <prop:complement-gap>

#proof[
  Choose smooth real functions $theta_0 \, theta_1 \, dots.h \, theta_m$ with
  $ sum_(a = 0)^m theta_a^2 = 1 \, #h(2em) theta_i = 1 med upright("on ") r_i lt.eq D \/ 5 \, #h(2em) "supp" theta_i subset.eq { r_i lt.eq D \/ 4 } . $
  The balls of radius $D \/ 4$ are disjoint by (5.3). In the annulus $D \/ 5 < r_i < D \/ 4$, take $theta_i = cos f \( r_i \/ D \)$ and $theta_0 = sin f \( r_i \/ D \)$, where $f$ is a fixed smooth transition from zero to $pi \/ 2$, constant near both endpoints. Set $theta_0 = 0$ in the inner balls and one outside the outer balls. At most one transition annulus is present at any point, so
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.12)")
   $ sum_(a = 0)^m \| nabla theta_a \|^2 lt.eq C D^(- 2) . $ <eq:continuum-ims-gradients>]
  This construction also verifies smoothness at the boundaries.
  Let $u in H^1 \( bb(R)^3 \; bb(C)^2 \)$, $P u = 0$, and $parallel u parallel = 1$. In spin component $sigma$, global orthogonality implies
  $ b_(i sigma) := chevron.l psi_i \, theta_i u_sigma chevron.r = chevron.l \( theta_i - 1 \) psi_i \, u_sigma chevron.r . $
  The tail estimate, integrated over $r_i gt.eq D \/ 5$, gives
  $ parallel \( theta_i - 1 \) psi_i parallel lt.eq C "poly" \( D \) e^(- .99 \( .2 - .01 \) D) lt.eq e^(- .18 D) . $
  Here the polynomial comes only from radial integration and has fixed degree. Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.13)")
   $ sum_(i \, sigma) \| b_(i sigma) \|^2 lt.eq m e^(- .36 D) . $ <eq:continuum-local-ground-leakage>]
  There is no assumption that a localized vector is exactly orthogonal to the local ground state.
  On $"supp" theta_i$, every $k eq.not i$ satisfies
  $ r_k gt.eq D - 2 S - D \/ 4 = 3 D \/ 4 - 2 S > D \/ 8 . $
  Hence $chi_k = 1$ throughout that support and $h = h_i$ there. Choose a fixed $0 < g_(upright("loc")) lt.eq min \( g_(upright(w)) \/ 2 \, 1 \/ 4 \)$. Equations (5.2) and (5.1), for large $D$, imply
  $ chevron.l theta_i u \, A theta_i u chevron.r & gt.eq g_(upright("loc")) parallel theta_i u parallel^2 - \( g_(upright("loc")) + e^(- 1.01 D) \) sum_sigma \| b_(i sigma) \|^2 . $
  On $"supp" theta_0$, each primary distance is at least $D \/ 5$. Each secondary distance is at least $D \/ 5 - rho_i gt.eq D \/ 5 - 4 gt.eq D \/ 6$. Since $q + q' lt.eq 2$ for large $D$, the magnitude of the full attraction on this support is at most $C m \/ D$. Thus
  $ chevron.l theta_0 u \, A theta_0 u chevron.r gt.eq \( 1 \/ 2 - C m \/ D \) parallel theta_0 u parallel^2 gt.eq g_(upright("loc")) parallel theta_0 u parallel^2 . $
  The kinetic product rule yields the IMS identity
  $ chevron.l u \, A u chevron.r = sum_(a = 0)^m chevron.l theta_a u \, A theta_a u chevron.r - 1 / 2 integral sum_(a = 0)^m \| nabla theta_a \|^2 \| u \|^2 . $
  It holds first for smooth functions and then on $H^1$ by continuity of the Coulomb forms. Summing the preceding bounds and using (5.12) and (5.13) gives
  $ chevron.l u \, A u chevron.r gt.eq g_(upright("loc")) - C m e^(- .36 D) - C D^(- 2) gt.eq g_(upright("loc")) \/ 2 . $
  Taking $g = g_(upright("loc")) \/ 2$ proves the proposition. ◻
]

=== 5.3 Uniform many-fermion bounds and elimination of high modes <uniform-many-fermion-bounds-and-elimination-of-high-modes>

Let

$ frak(H)_m = and.big^m frak(h) \, #h(2em) Pi = and.big^m P \, #h(2em) Q_m = I - Pi . $

Here $Pi$ projects onto #emph[all] $m$-electron configurations in the $2 m$ spin orbitals; it does not impose single occupancy or a total-spin condition. Denote second quantization on this fixed particle-number space by $d Gamma \( B \) = sum_(ell = 1)^m B^(\( ell \))$. In the scaled coordinates, write $W_(upright("ee")) = sum_(1 lt.eq ell < k lt.eq m) \| y_ell - y_k \|^(- 1)$ for the electron repulsion, acting trivially on spin.

Under the unitary dilation $y_ell = Z_(*) x_ell$, the physical Hamiltonian obeys the exact relation

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.14)")
 $ H / Z_(*)^2 = d Gamma \( h \) + W_(upright("ee")) / Z_(*) . $ <eq:continuum-exact-dilation>]

We henceforth use this unitary identification and set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.15)")
 $ cal(H) = H / Z_(*)^2 + m / 2 = d Gamma \( A \) + W_(upright("ee")) / Z_(*) . $ <eq:continuum-scaled-hamiltonian>]

To compare its spectral infimum with its compression to $Pi$, we will bound the gap on $Q_m$ and the coupling $Q_m cal(H) Pi$. The one-particle estimates control the contribution from $d Gamma \( A \)$; the following Hardy estimate controls the repulsion on every vector in $"Ran" Pi$.

We briefly specify the domains used below. On the antisymmetric subspace, the kinetic form domain is $H^1$ in all $3 m$ coordinates, with its finite spin components. Its operator domain is $H^2$ in those coordinates. Translated Hardy inequalities in one particle coordinate show that each nuclear or pair Coulomb multiplier is bounded from $H^1$ to $L^2$. For a fixed instance their finite sum therefore satisfies $parallel V u parallel_2 lt.eq C_(upright("inst")) parallel u parallel_(H^1)$. The Fourier inequality $parallel nabla u parallel_2 lt.eq epsilon parallel Delta u parallel_2 + C_epsilon parallel u parallel_2$ makes the potential infinitesimally operator-bounded relative to the total Laplacian. The Coulomb operator consequently has the indicated $H^2$ operator domain and $H^1$ form domain. The same reasoning applies to the one-particle operators. Every $omega_i$ is in $H^2 \( bb(R)^3 \)$. Products of these functions have square-integrable pure second derivatives and mixed first derivatives, so every vector in $"Ran" Pi$ belongs to the many-particle operator domain. Both $Pi$ and $Q_m$ preserve the form domain.

#thm([Lemma 5.3.])[
  (A dimension-independent fermionic Hardy bound).  #emph[Uniformly over all normalized $u in "Ran" Pi$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.16)")
 $ sum_(ell = 1)^m parallel nabla_(y_ell) u parallel_2^2 lt.eq C m \, #h(2em) parallel W_(upright("ee")) Pi parallel lt.eq C m^2 . $ <eq:continuum-fermion-hardy>]

 The constants do not depend on the dimension $binom(2 m, m)$ of $"Ran" Pi$.]
] <lem:fermion-hardy>

#proof[
  Let $\( K_(upright("raw")) \)_(i j) = chevron.l nabla psi_i \, nabla psi_j chevron.r$. This is positive semidefinite, with trace at most $C m$. In the orthonormal spatial modes the kinetic matrix is
  $ K_0 = G^(- 1 \/ 2) K_(upright("raw")) G^(- 1 \/ 2) . $
  Hence
  $ "Tr" K_0 = "Tr" \( K_(upright("raw")) G^(- 1) \) lt.eq parallel G^(- 1) parallel "Tr" K_(upright("raw")) lt.eq C m . $
  The spin matrix $K = K_0 ⊗ I_2$ has the same bound after adjusting the constant.
  For any normalized fermionic $u$, its one-particle density matrix $gamma$ in these modes satisfies $0 lt.eq gamma lt.eq I$. Indeed, for a coefficient vector $v$, the quadratic form of $gamma$ is $parallel c \( v \) u parallel^2$; the anticommutation relation $c \( v \) c \( v \)^dagger + c \( v \)^dagger c \( v \) = parallel v parallel^2 I$ places it between zero and $parallel v parallel^2$. Thus, for an arbitrary superposition in the finite-mode space,
  $ sum_ell parallel nabla_(y_ell) u parallel_2^2 = "Tr" \( gamma K \) lt.eq "Tr" K lt.eq C m . $
  For a pair $ell < k$, applying the three-dimensional Hardy inequality in $y_ell$, with $y_k$ and all other variables held fixed, gives
  $ parallel thin \| y_ell - y_k \|^(- 1) u parallel_2 lt.eq 2 parallel nabla_(y_ell) u parallel_2 . $
  Integration over the remaining variables and spin components justifies this inequality for the full wavefunction. Put $b_m = m \( m - 1 \) \/ 2$. Cauchy–Schwarz in the sum of pair multipliers gives, when $m gt.eq 2$,
  $ parallel W_(upright("ee")) u parallel_2^2 & lt.eq b_m sum_(ell < k) parallel thin \| y_ell - y_k \|^(- 1) u parallel_2^2\
 & lt.eq 4 b_m sum_(ell < k) parallel nabla_(y_ell) u parallel_2^2\
 & lt.eq 4 b_m \( m - 1 \) sum_ell parallel nabla_(y_ell) u parallel_2^2 lt.eq C m^4 . $
  For $m = 1$ the repulsion is zero. This proves the operator bound on the whole finite-mode space, rather than only on its individual Slater determinants. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 5.4.])[
  (Eliminating the many-particle complement).  #emph[For sufficiently large $D$, let $E_Pi = min "Spec" \( Pi cal(H) Pi \|_("Ran" Pi) \)$. Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.17)")
 $ 0 lt.eq E_Pi - inf "Spec" cal(H) lt.eq e^(- 1.2 D) . $ <eq:continuum-leakage-error>]

 More explicitly, the upper bound can be taken to be 
$ C (m^(3 \/ 2) e^(- .75 D) + m^2 \/ Z_(*))^2 . $

]
] <lem:continuum-elimination>

#proof[
  Let $epsilon = parallel A P parallel lt.eq C sqrt(m) thin e^(- .75 D)$. The one-particle off-block operator
  $ R_(upright("off")) = P A \( I - P \) + \( I - P \) A P $
  extends to a bounded self-adjoint operator, of norm $parallel \( I - P \) A P parallel lt.eq epsilon$. The adjoint identifies the two blocks even though $A$ itself is unbounded. Its block-diagonal remainder satisfies
  $ A - R_(upright("off")) = P A P + \( I - P \) A \( I - P \) gt.eq - epsilon P + g \( I - P \) $
  by Proposition 24. Consequently, on the full many-particle form domain,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.18)")
   $ d Gamma \( A \) gt.eq g N_perp - 2 m epsilon I \, #h(2em) N_perp = d Gamma \( I - P \) . $ <eq:continuum-high-number-bound>]
  Here the low diagonal can cost at most $m epsilon$, and $parallel d Gamma \( R_(upright("off")) \) parallel lt.eq m epsilon$. The commuting one-particle projections $\( I - P \)^(\( ell \))$ show that $N_perp$ has nonnegative integer eigenvalues, with kernel exactly $"Ran" Pi$. Therefore $N_perp gt.eq Q_m$, including on superpositions of states with different numbers of high-mode particles.
  The repulsion form is nonnegative on every wavefunction, so its compression to $Q_m$ is nonnegative even though $W_(upright("ee"))$ need not commute with $Pi$. Equation (5.18) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.19)")
   $ Q_m cal(H) Q_m gt.eq \( g - 2 m epsilon \) Q_m gt.eq c Q_m \, #h(2em) c = g \/ 2 \, $ <eq:continuum-many-body-gap>]
  for large $D$.
  By Lemma 25, the coupling from the finite subspace has operator norm at most
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.20)")
   $ beta := parallel Q_m cal(H) Pi parallel lt.eq m epsilon + frac(C m^2, Z_(*)) lt.eq C m^(3 \/ 2) e^(- .75 D) + frac(C m^2, Z_(*)) . $ <eq:continuum-coupling>]
  The first term follows because the block-diagonal one-particle sum preserves $"Ran" Pi$. The compressed operator also obeys
  $ parallel Pi cal(H) Pi parallel lt.eq m epsilon + C m^2 \/ Z_(*) = o \( 1 \) . $
  For any form-domain vector $u = p + v$, where $p = Pi u$ and $v = Q_m u$, the finite-mode operator-domain inclusion and the bounded coupling justify
  $ chevron.l u \, cal(H) u chevron.r & gt.eq chevron.l p \, cal(H) p chevron.r + c parallel v parallel^2 - 2 beta parallel p parallel parallel v parallel\
 & gt.eq chevron.l p \, cal(H) p chevron.r - frac(2 beta^2, c) parallel p parallel^2 + c / 2 parallel v parallel^2 . $
  Equivalently, this is the form inequality
  $ cal(H) gt.eq Pi cal(H) Pi - frac(2 beta^2, c) Pi + c / 2 Q_m . $
  Since the finite block has norm $o \( 1 \)$, the bottom of the right side is $E_Pi - 2 beta^2 \/ c$ for large $D$. The opposite variational inequality follows by restricting test vectors to $"Ran" Pi$. This proves the explicit error bound. In the present regime, with $Z_(*) gt.eq e^D \/ 2$, it is at most $e^(- 1.2 D)$. All lower bounds apply to quadratic forms and hence to spectral infima; existence of an eigenvector at the bottom of the full continuum spectrum is not used. ◻
]

In particular, the explicit error in this lemma, after multiplication by $Z_(*)$, is bounded by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.21)")
 $ C (m^3 e^(- .5 D) + m^(7 \/ 2) e^(- .75 D) + m^4 e^(- D)) . $ <eq:continuum-leakage-final-units>]

The weaker bound $Z_(*) e^(- 1.2 D) lt.eq 2 e^(- .2 D)$ will suffice. This squaring of the residual against the fixed complement gap is what permits the final energy amplification.

=== 5.4 The compressed Coulomb interaction <the-compressed-coulomb-interaction>

The full-space energy is now controlled by its finite-mode compression, with an error that remains small after multiplication by $Z_(*)$. To identify that compression we retain the direct intersite repulsion and estimate it explicitly. At the present exponentially amplified scales it has norm $O \( m^2 \/ D \)$ in Hubbard units, unlike constructions that must keep it at leading order at polynomial confinement scales.

Identify $"Ran" Pi$ with $and.big^m \( bb(C)^m ⊗ bb(C)^2 \)$ using the isometry $cal(J)_m = and.big^m cal(J)$. Let

$ W_(upright("eff")) = cal(J)_m^(*) W_(upright("ee")) cal(J)_m . $

Creation and annihilation operators below refer to these orthonormal abstract site modes.

#thm([Lemma 5.5.])[
  (Coulomb coefficients in the site modes).  #emph[For sufficiently large $D$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.22)")
 $ ∥W_(upright("eff")) - U sum_(i = 1)^m n_(i arrow.t) n_(i arrow.b)∥ lt.eq frac(C m^5, D) \, #h(2em) U = 5 / 8 . $ <eq:continuum-coulomb-error>]

]
] <lem:continuum-coulomb-compression>

#proof[
  For four spatial functions define
  $ cal(V) \( a \, b \; c \, d \) = integral.double frac(overline(a \( x \) b \( y \)) thin c \( x \) d \( y \), \| x - y \|) thin thin upright(d) x thin thin upright(d) y . $
  Hardy in the $x$ coordinate and Cauchy–Schwarz show that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.23)")
   $ \| cal(V) \( a \, b \; c \, d \) \| lt.eq 2 parallel a parallel_2 parallel b parallel_2 parallel nabla c parallel_2 parallel d parallel_2 . $ <eq:continuum-coefficient-continuity>]
  Telescoping a change of the four arguments therefore makes this coefficient Lipschitz in their $H^1$ norms on uniformly bounded $H^1$ sets. The same statement holds for fixed spin orbitals, with the usual spin Kronecker factors.
  The coefficients of $W_(upright("eff"))$ are these integrals in the modes $omega_i$ and their two spin copies. Equation (5.7) permits their replacement by $phi.alt_i$ with error at most $C m \/ D$ per coefficient. Indeed all four $H^1$ norms stay uniformly bounded. In the standard second-quantized expression each coefficient multiplies $1 / 2 c_a^dagger c_b^dagger c_d c_c$, a monomial of norm at most one. There are at most $\( 2 m \)^4$ coefficients. Thus this replacement changes the operator by at most $C m^5 \/ D$. We are comparing coefficient arrays in the fixed abstract orthonormal site space; no orthogonality of the replacement functions $phi.alt_i$ is asserted or needed.
  Choose a fixed smooth radial cutoff equal to one on $r_i lt.eq D \/ 5$, zero on $r_i gt.eq D \/ 4$, and with gradient bounded by $C \/ D$, and let $zeta_i$ be its product with $phi.alt_i$. Direct integration of the hydrogenic tail gives
  $ parallel zeta_i - phi.alt_i parallel_(H^1) lt.eq C "poly" \( D \) e^(- D \/ 5) lt.eq e^(- .18 D) . $
  The supports of the $zeta_i$ are disjoint. By (5.23), replacing all $phi.alt_i$ by $zeta_i$ changes the coefficient operator by at most $C m^4 e^(- .18 D)$.
  For the truncated functions a nonzero coefficient must have the same site label for both factors at $x$, and likewise at $y$. Therefore the only surviving terms are onsite and intersite density terms. If $i eq.not j$, their spatial coefficient satisfies
  $ 0 lt.eq V_(i j)^(upright("dir")) = integral.double frac(\| zeta_i \( x \) \|^2 \| zeta_j \( y \) \|^2, \| x - y \|) thin thin upright(d) x thin thin upright(d) y lt.eq frac(1, D \/ 2 - 2 S) lt.eq C / D \, $
  using $parallel zeta_i parallel_2 lt.eq 1$. The intersite operator is $sum_(i < j) V_(i j)^(upright("dir")) n_i n_j$, where $n_i = n_(i arrow.t) + n_(i arrow.b)$ has norm two. It consequently has norm at most $C m^2 \/ D$.
  The onsite spatial integral differs from $cal(V) \( phi.alt_i \, phi.alt_i \; phi.alt_i \, phi.alt_i \) = U$ by at most $C e^(- .18 D)$. The two equal-spin monomials vanish by anticommutation. The two opposite-spin monomials, together with the factor $1 \/ 2$ in the interaction, give exactly $U n_(i arrow.t) n_(i arrow.b)$. Combining these estimates yields
  $ ∥W_(upright("eff")) - U sum_i n_(i arrow.t) n_(i arrow.b)∥ lt.eq C (m^5 / D + m^4 e^(- .18 D) + m^2 / D) lt.eq frac(C m^5, D) . $
  ◻
]

=== 5.5 The simulation estimate in physical energy units <the-simulation-estimate-in-physical-energy-units>

#thm([Proposition 5.6.])[
  (Simulation of the Hubbard spectral minimum).  #emph[Let $E_0 = inf "Spec" H$ for the physical molecular Hamiltonian and let $E_(upright("Hub")) \( t \)$ be the minimum of the half-filled Hubbard Hamiltonian in Proposition 7. The construction satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.24)")
 $ lr(|E_0 / Z_(*) + frac(m Z_(*), 2) - E_(upright("Hub")) \( t \)|) & lt.eq m delta_1 + frac(C m^5, D) + Z_(*) e^(- 1.2 D)\
 & lt.eq C #scale(x: 240%, y: 240%)[\[] m^2 epsilon.alt_(upright("hop")) + m^5 / D + m e^(- .01 D) + m^2 e^(- .1 D)\
 & #h(35mm)   + m^2 e^(- .6 D) + e^(- .2 D) #scale(x: 240%, y: 240%)[\]] . $ <eq:continuum-simulation-units>]

 In particular, for every prescribed fixed inverse-polynomial error budget $epsilon.alt_(*)$, the polynomial scale and calibration precision can be chosen so that the left side is at most $epsilon.alt_(*)$.]
] <prop:continuum-simulation>

#proof[
  The exact compression, in the abstract site space, is
  $ cal(J)_m^(*) \( Z_(*) cal(H) \) cal(J)_m = d Gamma #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] Z_(*) cal(J)^(*) A cal(J) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + W_(upright("eff")) . $
  The Hubbard hopping term is $d Gamma \( sans(T) ⊗ I_2 \)$. On $m$ particles, second quantization of a bounded one-particle difference increases its norm by at most a factor $m$. Lemma 23 and Lemma 27 therefore give
  $ ∥cal(J)_m^(*) \( Z_(*) cal(H) \) cal(J)_m - H_(upright("Hub")) \( t \)∥ lt.eq m delta_1 + C m^5 \/ D . $
  For finite self-adjoint matrices, the difference of their spectral minima is bounded by the operator norm difference, by the variational principle. Lemma 26 adds at most $Z_(*) e^(- 1.2 D)$ when the restriction to $"Ran" Pi$ is removed. Finally, (5.15) gives the exact energy identity
  $ Z_(*) inf "Spec" cal(H) = E_0 / Z_(*) + frac(m Z_(*), 2) . $
  This proves (5.24). Substitute (5.6) and $Z_(*) lt.eq 2 e^D$ to obtain (5.24).
  The right side involves only fixed polynomial factors in $m$, the calibrated hopping error, inverse powers of $D$, and the displayed decaying exponentials. Equation (5.4) and Proposition 22 allow the hopping error to be made an arbitrarily small fixed inverse polynomial. The simultaneous choices, their order, and polynomial bit cost are specified in Section 6. No selected-orbital assumption has been imposed on the spectral infimum in this comparison. ◻
]

== 6 Parameters, computation, and the final promise <sec:completion>

We now put the constructions in their algorithmic order. This matters because the tail formulas are proved for tuned wells, whereas the positions must be computed before the tuning is performed. The surrogate fields in 4 remove this apparent dependence.

=== 6.1 A uniform choice of scales <a-uniform-choice-of-scales>

Let $n gt.eq 2$ bound the source input size, with the fixed polynomial magnitude and inverse-gap bounds from 2. Write $E_(upright("in")) = E \( H_(upright("in")) \)$. Put

$ delta_s = b_s - a_s \, #h(2em) gamma = min { 1 \, delta_s } \, #h(2em) alpha = eta^2 \/ U . $

Use the scales and rational hopping approximation of 7. They give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.1)")
 $ lr(|E_(upright("Hub")) \( t \) - alpha \( E_(upright("in")) - C_s \)|) lt.eq frac(3 alpha gamma, 64) \, #h(2em) C_s = 3 k Delta + 3 / 2 sum_e v_e^2 + sum_({ i \, j } in cal(C)) K_(i j) . $ <eq:finite-final-error>]

All parameters chosen so far have polynomial magnitude or inverse-polynomial lower bounds as appropriate. Their binary descriptions have polynomial length. The scale $alpha$ is rational. The scalar $C_s$ is a sum of polynomially many efficiently computable square roots and rational terms; it does not depend on $E_(upright("in"))$.

Set the requested continuum error to

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.2)")
 $ epsilon.alt_(*) = frac(alpha gamma, 64) . $ <eq:continuum-budget>]

We verify that a single deterministic polynomial choice of $D$ meets this budget. The calibrated displacement bound has the form

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.3)")
 $ S lt.eq C n^b \( 1 + log D \) $ <eq:uniform-displacement-size>]

for a fixed $b$, since $m$, the number of contacts, and $\| log t_(i j) \|$ are polynomially bounded in $n$. Increase $b$ so that $m lt.eq C n^b$ as well. The hopping error before polynomial norm conversions is bounded by a constant times

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.4)")
 $ frak(e) \( D \, tau \) =  & frac(m log D + m^2, D) + frac(m S, D) + S^2 / D + S D^(- 1 \/ 4)\
 & + tau + e^(- c sqrt(D)) . $ <eq:calibration-budget>]

The other errors are $"poly" \( m \) \/ D$ and the exponentially small terms listed below. Choose fixed exponents $h \, r$ so that all polynomial norm-conversion and coefficient-counting losses are bounded by $C n^h$, and $epsilon.alt_(*) gt.eq c n^(- r)$. In particular, take $m^2 + m^5 lt.eq C n^h$; the two algebraic error terms in (5.24) are then bounded by $C n^h \( frak(e) \( D \, tau \) + D^(- 1) \)$. Such exponents exist by the explicit finite construction. Increasing them only weakens the conditions to be met.

Choose an integer $K$ satisfying

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.5)")
 $ K > 4 \( b + h + r + 2 \) \, $ <eq:choice-K>]

and put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.6)")
 $ D = A \( n + 2 \)^K \, #h(2em) tau = c_(*) \( n + 2 \)^(- h - r - 2) \, $ <eq:choice-D>]

where $A$ is a sufficiently large fixed integer and $c_(*) > 0$ a sufficiently small fixed rational. The factors $1 + log D$ in (6.3) do not make this choice circular: $K$ is chosen first, and then $A$ is fixed. Each $D$-dependent expression tends to zero relative to its error budget as $A$ increases, even with its powers of $log A$. The remaining growth in $n$ is controlled by (6.5). In particular,

$ n^h S D^(- 1 \/ 4) = o \( n^(- r) \) \, #h(2em) n^h S^2 \/ D = o \( n^(- r) \) \, #h(2em) m + S = o \( D^(1 \/ 4) \) . $

Here the little-oh statements describe the uniform large-scale estimates used to choose the fixed constants; increasing $A$ covers every finite small-$n$ case. The same choices bound all the terms in (6.4) and the Coulomb-compression error by their assigned fractions of (6.2). No search for a successful $D$ is part of the algorithm.

It is important to keep the energy units explicit. After dilation and subtraction of the isolated-well ground energy,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.7)")
 $ cal(H) = H \/ Z_(*)^2 + m \/ 2 \, #h(2em) Z_(*) cal(H) = H \/ Z_(*) + m Z_(*) \/ 2 . $ <eq:units-final>]

The exponential margins that are needed after multiplication by $Z_(*) asymp e^D$ are:

